textosLlegan("theory", {"fil-t6":{"html":"<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>How to study this topic: politics is not just what parties do, but the question of <strong>how we should organise life in common</strong>. Two questions organise the whole topic: why does the <strong>State</strong> exist and what makes it <strong>legitimate</strong>? And what is a <strong>just</strong> society? The table of the three contract theorists (Hobbes, Locke, Rousseau) is the best guide for revision.</p> </blockquote>\n<h2>1. The human being, a social and political animal</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-3\">We do not live alone: we are born, we grow up and we become persons within a community. That is why <strong>Aristotle</strong> defined the human being as <strong>zoon politikon</strong>, ‘political animal’, one who lives in the <em>polis</em> (the city). Whoever could live entirely in isolation, he said, ‘is either a beast or a god’. <strong>Politics</strong>, in its philosophical sense, is reflection on how to organise that life in common: power, laws, justice and the best way of governing ourselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Political philosophy</strong> is not limited to describing what societies are like (sociology or political science do that); it asks how they <strong>should</strong> be: which power is legitimate, which distribution is fair, what freedom we should have. It is a branch of practical philosophy, a sister of ethics.</p>\n<h3>The classics: Plato and Aristotle</h3>\n<p>Political philosophy is born in Greece with two opposing answers. <strong>Plato</strong>, in the <em>Republic</em>, starts from a parallel: the just city is understood as a soul writ large. Just as the soul has three parts —reason, spirit and desire—, the city has three classes: the <strong>rulers</strong> (who know the good), the <strong>guardians</strong> (who defend it) and the <strong>producers</strong> (who supply it). <strong>Justice</strong> does not consist in everyone being equal, but in each part fulfilling its function without encroaching on the others': a harmony like the health of the body. Hence his most controversial thesis: the one who knows should rule, the philosopher, the only one able to leave the cave and look upon the good. So that power does not corrupt, he even proposes that rulers and guardians have neither property nor family of their own. Today we object that this is a dangerous elitism —who watches those who know?—, and it remains an open debate: is politics a matter for experts or for everyone?</p>\n<p><strong>Aristotle</strong>, his pupil, corrects this idealism with an eye more attentive to the facts. For him, the human being is by nature a <em>zoon politikón</em> (a 'political animal'): only in the community, thanks to language and reason, does he develop a fully human life, and the <strong>polis</strong> is the end point of that natural development. That is why politics is not separate from ethics: its end is <strong>eudaimonia</strong>, the good life. Aristotle classifies regimes by two criteria, how many govern and in whose interest: they are <strong>right</strong> when they seek the common good (monarchy, aristocracy and polity) and <strong>deviant</strong> when they seek the private interest of the ruler (tyranny, oligarchy and demagogy). Against Plato, he defends private property, because it makes each person more responsible and careful, and he proposes as the most stable regime the <strong>polity</strong>, resting on a broad middle class that moderates the extremes. The tug-of-war stays open: rule of the best or rule of the many?</p>\n<h3>Power and God: medieval politics</h3>\n<p>For centuries, politics was thought from theology, even if practice clashed with earthly reality. <strong>Augustine of Hippo</strong>, in <em>The City of God</em>, distinguished two communities intertwined in history: the <strong>city of God</strong>, moved by the love of God, and the <strong>earthly city</strong>, moved by self-love. Politics, in this view, is needed to curb disorder and violence, but it cannot bring salvation or the perfect society: it is an instrument, not the ultimate end. With this, Augustine lowers the hopes placed in any human power.</p>\n<p><strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong>, already in the thirteenth century, offers an architecture of law on several levels: <strong>eternal law</strong> (the divine order of the world), <strong>natural law</strong> (the part of that order that human reason can know by itself), <strong>human or positive law</strong> (the concrete laws of each community) and <strong>divine law</strong> (the revealed law). The decisive point is that natural law gives morality and politics a <strong>rational</strong> basis, not only a religious one: that is why anyone, believer or not, can argue about what is just. The end of the state is the <strong>common good</strong>; when a ruler betrays it and falls into tyranny, he loses his legitimacy, and in certain cases he may be resisted. It is a clear forerunner of the right of rebellion that Locke would defend centuries later.</p>\n<h2>2. Why does the State exist? Nature versus pact</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-6\">The <strong>State</strong> is the organisation that exercises power over a territory: it makes laws, administers justice and holds the legitimate use of force. Where does it get that power? There are two main answers. For the ancients (Aristotle), the political community is <strong>natural</strong>: it springs from our own social condition. For the moderns, by contrast, the State is an <strong>artifice</strong>, something that human beings create through an agreement: the <strong>social contract</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Cómo funciona el Estado: Weber y la violencia legítima monopolizada</h3><p>Si vamos más allá de una definición breve, descubrimos que el <strong>Estado</strong> moderno posee una característica que lo distingue de toda otra organización humana. Weber la expresó con gran exactitud: se trata de aquella institución que, en un territorio concreto, logra reservarse eficazmente el <strong>monopolio de la violencia física legítima</strong>. Esta definición no afirma que el Estado actúe de forma violenta; afirma que únicamente él tiene capacidad para autorizar la fuerza, a través de cuerpos policiales, jueces o fuerzas armadas, mientras que toda coacción ajena queda en principio al margen de la legalidad. Casi nunca necesita emplearla realmente: es suficiente con que la ciudadanía sepa que podría recurrir a ella. Por esta razón Weber vinculó siempre el poder con la <strong>legitimidad</strong>: no acatamos las órdenes únicamente por miedo a una sanción, sino porque consideramos que esa autoridad resulta de algún modo justificada y no un simple antojo.</p><p>Dentro del Estado moderno, dicha legitimidad se apoya principalmente en la <strong>ley</strong>, no en quien ocupa el mando. Lo que acatamos es la función, sea la de un alcalde, un juez o un empleado público, siempre que respete las normas, y no a la persona concreta. Weber denominó a este tipo de dominación <strong>racional-legal</strong> y la asoció a la estructura <strong>burocrática</strong>, que proporciona a la administración un funcionamiento previsible y un tratamiento igualitario, si bien también recibe críticas por resultar fría e impersonal. Se aleja notablemente tanto de las monarquías apoyadas en la tradición como del liderazgo basado en el carisma de un individuo.</p><p>En el ámbito jurídico es habitual distinguir tres <strong>elementos</strong> estatales. El <strong>territorio</strong> corresponde al espacio material, delimitado por fronteras, en el que se aplican sus normas. La <strong>población</strong> la forman las personas que quedan sujetas a dicha autoridad. Por último, la <strong>soberanía</strong> consiste en un poder supremo que no admite ninguno superior: en el interior, supone la decisión definitiva sobre sus habitantes; en el exterior, la igualdad formal frente al resto de Estados. Si alguno de estos tres componentes está ausente, no puede hablarse plenamente de Estado.</p><p>También es importante distinguir entre <strong>Estado</strong> y <strong>gobierno</strong>. Este último es el grupo determinado que ostenta el poder en cierto momento y que se renueva mediante elecciones; aquel es el entramado estable de normas, instituciones y empleados públicos que perdura bajo esos cambios. Los gobiernos caen, mientras que el Estado continúa. Gracias a esta diferencia comprendemos que sustituir un gobierno no equivale a transformar el Estado, y también que las principales luchas políticas acostumbran a centrarse en el control de esa maquinaria permanente.</p><p>No obstante, desde hace bastante la soberanía ya no es ilimitada. Hacia arriba, los Estados transfieren algunas de sus atribuciones a <strong>organismos supraestatales</strong>, como la <strong>ONU</strong>, creada en 1945 con el fin de evitar otros conflictos bélicos y de coordinar a las naciones, o la <strong>Unión Europea</strong>, cuyos miembros comparten una moneda, unos tribunales y gran parte de sus leyes. Hacia abajo, en el interior de cada Estado coexisten entidades <strong>infraestatales</strong>, como comunidades autónomas, regiones o ayuntamientos, que disponen de instituciones y atribuciones propias. Así pues, la distribución efectiva del poder resulta mucho más compleja que la idea de un Estado uniforme y aislado.</p>\n<h3>Is the nation discovered or constructed? Two great theories</h3><p>If the state is a legal reality, the <strong>nation</strong> is rather a community of people who feel bound by a shared history and culture. But where does that sense of belonging come from? Here two great families of answers clash. The first is <strong>essentialism</strong> or <strong>primordialism</strong>: nations would be ancient, almost natural realities, there from time immemorial waiting to awaken; each people would have a soul or character of its own, prior to any political decision. The second is <strong>constructivism</strong> (also called modernism): nations are not discovered, they are constructed; they are a relatively recent product, tied to the modern world, and for that very reason can change and be remade over time.</p><p>Three authors shaped the constructivist answer and are worth knowing. <strong>Benedict Anderson</strong> defined the nation as an <strong>imagined community</strong>: imagined, because its members will never come to know one another and yet think of themselves as brothers; community, because it is lived as a comradeship among equals. For Anderson it was printing and the press in everyday languages that let millions of strangers picture themselves at once as a single <em>we</em> that reads the same newspapers and shares the same present. <strong>Ernest Gellner</strong> stressed <strong>industrialisation</strong>: the modern economy needed mobile, literate workers with a common culture, and it was above all the state school that manufactured that homogeneity; hence his provocative thesis that it is not the nation that breeds nationalism, but nationalism that invents the nation. <strong>Eric Hobsbawm</strong>, finally, studied the <strong>invention of tradition</strong>: many customs presented as age-old —anthems, flags, parades, 'traditional' costumes— were created or reinvented in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries to give nations a venerable past and an air of eternity.</p><p>Against this current, those who defend continuity recall that languages, inhabited territories and collective memories persist across centuries and are not improvised by decree: reducing the nation to a pure invention seems to them to overlook something that many live as deeply real. The clash between the two positions is far from settled.</p><p>The two theories are not mere academic curiosity: they imply different policies. If the nation is something given and eternal, defending it may lead to shielding its supposed purity; if it is constructed, one may ask who constructs it, to what ends and whom it leaves out. Today most scholars lean towards some form of constructivism, though few deny that national constructions rest on real materials —languages, territories, memories— that are not infinitely malleable.</p>\n<h3>Civic nation, ethnic nation and constitutional patriotism</h3><p>Within this debate a distinction has become classic between two ways of understanding who belongs to a nation. The <strong>ethnic nation</strong> (or cultural) defines the member by what they <em>are</em>: origin, ancestry, mother tongue, inherited belonging; one is born into it and it is hard to enter from outside. The <strong>civic nation</strong> (or political) defines the member by what they <em>accept</em>: the laws, the rights and the rules of common life; it is a community of citizens that, in principle, anyone can join. The first looks towards a received past; the second, towards an agreement renewed in the present. In reality almost no nation is pure: all mix features of both types. But the emphasis one places —on heritage or on consent— greatly changes who stays in and who out, which is why the distinction serves better as a compass than as a rigid label.</p><p><strong>Jürgen Habermas</strong> proposed a way out of this tension: <strong>constitutional patriotism</strong>. His idea is that, in plural and mixed societies, the common bond should not rest on a prior ethnic identity, but on shared allegiance to the <strong>principles</strong> of a democratic constitution: liberty, equality, respect for rights. Thus people of very different origins can feel part of one political project without giving up their own culture. It is a loyalty to rules of justice, and not to a blood or a lineage.</p><p>All this takes on special relief in <strong>plurinational states</strong>, where several nations coexist under one legal roof. There the question arises of how to share out self-government, which languages to recognise as official or how to fit together loyalties that do not always coincide. There is no single formula: federalism (as in Switzerland or Canada), the state of autonomies or the recognition of several official languages are some of the historical attempts to let diversity fit within one state without breaking it. Each arrangement has its defenders and critics, and its balance is revised again and again.</p><p>History offers two background images of these models. The tradition that starts from the French Revolution conceived the nation as a body of citizens equal before the law, regardless of their origin or blood; German romanticism, by contrast, with authors like Herder, stressed the spirit of the people, its language and its culture as an inherited collective soul. From these two roots, much intertwined, almost all later nationalisms drink, which explains why the same movement can appeal now to rights and now to roots.</p>\n<p>Contract theorists imagine a <strong>state of nature</strong>—what life would be like <em>without</em> political power—in order to justify why it is worth leaving it. It is not a historical fact, but a hypothesis for thinking about the foundations of power. Three authors give three different answers.</p>\n<h3>An open debate: identity, neutrality and the Basque case</h3><p>Theories of the nation do not close the political discussion: rather they reopen it with better tools. Two questions sum it up. The first is: <strong>is the nation discovered or constructed?</strong> Those who lean towards primordialism stress that a language, an inhabited landscape and a shared memory are not invented overnight, and that treating every identity as a mere artifice can sound like denying something people feel as their own and very real. Those who follow Anderson, Gellner or Hobsbawm reply that recognising that something is constructed makes it neither false nor less valuable: friendships, cities or languages are also built over time, and do not thereby cease to matter to us truly.</p><p>The second question is: <strong>can a state be neutral between the different identities?</strong> Liberalism aspires to a state that takes no side for any culture and treats all citizens equally. Its critics reply that complete neutrality is impossible: every state chooses one or more official languages, a calendar of holidays, a story of its own history, and with each of those choices it favours some and makes things harder for others. Habermas's constitutional patriotism is an attempt to answer that objection; communitarianism is another, in recalling that people are always formed within concrete cultures that deserve recognition, and not only tolerance.</p><p>In the <strong>Basque context</strong>, split on both sides of the Franco-Spanish border, these questions are not an abstract exercise: they touch the language, the degree of self-government, memory and the sense of belonging, and there are very different positions held with good reasons by equally honest people. Philosophy does not come here to dictate a verdict or to say who is right, but to offer the distinctions —state and nation, civic and ethnic, discover and construct— that allow it to be discussed calmly and respectfully, truly listening to the arguments on all sides.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Autor</th><th>Estado de naturaleza</th><th>El pacto</th><th>Estado que resulta</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Hobbes</strong></td><td>Cada individuo combate contra todos los demás. Hobbes da a conocer, en la dedicatoria de su obra <em>De cive</em> (1642), una expresión tomada de Plauto: ‘man is wolf to man’ (<em>Asinaria</em>, v. 495). Reinan el temor y la falta de seguridad.</td><td>El pánico a una muerte violenta lleva a que cada cual entregue su fuerza a un único soberano.</td><td><strong>Monarquía absoluta</strong> (el <em>Leviatán</em>): una autoridad poderosa que asegura la convivencia pacífica.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Locke</strong></td><td>Bastante tranquilo, aunque precario: las personas poseen <strong>derechos naturales</strong> (a conservar la existencia, a ser libres y a disponer de sus bienes), pero nadie actúa como árbitro neutral.</td><td>Acuerdo <strong>limitado</strong>: los ciudadanos, que deben dar su conformidad, entregan únicamente la facultad de juzgar y sancionar.</td><td><strong>Estado liberal</strong>: una autoridad con límites, en la que legislar y ejecutar quedan separados (la división en tres poderes la formulará después Montesquieu), y que reconoce a los ciudadanos la posibilidad de alzarse frente al tirano.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rousseau</strong></td><td>En su estado natural, la persona (a la que los libros de texto llaman ‘noble savage’, aunque Rousseau jamás emplea esa fórmula) vive libre y en igualdad; la corrupción procede de la <em>sociedad</em>, que introduce la propiedad y las diferencias entre unos y otros.</td><td>Cada individuo se asocia con el conjunto y se somete a la <strong>voluntad general</strong>, orientada al interés de todos.</td><td><strong>Soberanía popular</strong>: son los propios ciudadanos quienes se dan las leyes. De aquí nace la democracia actual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The idea of the contract also had its critics. <strong>Hume</strong> objected that real states almost always arose from <strong>conquest</strong> and usurpation, and are sustained by <strong>custom</strong>; nobody has signed this pact, and the supposed ‘tacit consent’ is unconvincing. We obey, rather, because without government society could not function: out of <strong>utility</strong>. <strong>Kant</strong>, by contrast, rescued the contract by reinterpreting it: it is not a historical fact but an <strong>idea of reason</strong> that serves as a criterion for judging laws. A law is just if a whole people <em>could</em> have accepted it.</p>\n<h3>A third answer: power comes from God</h3>\n<h3>Before the theories: why power had to be legitimised</h3>\n<p>Theories about the origin of power do not arise from nothing. For most of prehistory we lived in small groups of hunters and gatherers that were fairly <strong>egalitarian</strong>, with no kings and no great fortunes. With agriculture and settled life came the <strong>surplus</strong>: there was now something to accumulate, and with it the <strong>division of labour</strong> and the first hierarchies. The agrarian empires became highly stratified, with a minority ruling over a majority that worked. Every inequality that seeks to last needs a <strong>story that justifies it</strong>, and one of the most powerful was to say that power had been ordained by God.</p>\n<p>Between Aristotle’s <strong>natural</strong> theory and the moderns’ <strong>pact</strong> there was a doctrine that propped up Europe’s monarchies for centuries: the <strong>divine right of kings</strong>. According to it, power springs neither from our social nature nor from consent: it <strong>comes from God</strong>, who grants it directly to each monarch. Its starting point was a sentence of the Bible: ‘there is no authority except from God’ (<em>Epistle to the Romans</em> 13,1, St Paul).</p>\n<p>This idea had great theorists. The French bishop <strong>Jacques Bossuet</strong>, tutor to Louis XIV’s son, systematised it in <em>Politics Drawn from the Very Words of Holy Scripture</em> (1709, posthumous): the king receives power from God and answers only to God, so resisting the sovereign is opposing the divine order. A century earlier, <strong>James VI</strong> of Scotland (from 1603 also James I of England) had defended it in <em>The True Law of Free Monarchies</em> (1598): the monarch is accountable to God alone. Against all this, the <strong>contractualists</strong>—Hobbes, Locke and Rousseau—hold that power arises from a <strong>pact</strong> between human beings, and this contrast is easier to see in a table:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th></th><th>Divine right</th><th>Contractualism</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Where does power come from?</td><td>From <strong>God</strong>, who grants it to the monarch</td><td>From the <strong>consent</strong> of the people, who make a pact</td></tr>\n<tr><td>To whom is the ruler answerable?</td><td>To God alone</td><td>To the governed and to the law (except in Hobbes: his sovereign is not answerable to the subjects)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>May one resist?</td><td>No: to rebel is to disobey God</td><td>Yes, if the ruler breaks the pact (although Hobbes almost never admits it and Locke defends it)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The difference is not a small one: if power comes from God, the subject is not its owner and has nothing to agree to; if it comes from a pact, the ruler is, in principle, a <strong>trustee</strong> who can be held to account and, if need be, removed (Hobbes is the exception: for him, the pact cannot be undone). Modern democracies come out of this change of answer.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: today almost nobody defends divine right, but some leaders present themselves as ‘chosen by destiny’ or ‘the sole interpreters of the people’. Do they resemble James VI in any way?</p>\n\n<h2>3. Power and its legitimacy</h2>\n<p>It is worth distinguishing <strong>power</strong> (the capacity to make others obey) from <strong>legitimacy</strong> (the right to command, which makes us obey out of conviction and not just out of fear). A power is legitimate when those who obey recognise it as just. <strong>Max Weber</strong> distinguished three sources of legitimacy: <strong>tradition</strong> (‘it has always been done this way’), <strong>charisma</strong> (the personal strength of a leader) and <strong>rational-legal authority</strong> (laws are obeyed, not persons), characteristic of the modern State.</p>\n<h3>Power and authority</h3>\n<p><strong>Power</strong> is not an object kept in a drawer, but a <strong>relationship</strong>: it appears when someone gets another person to do something they would not do on their own. That is why it depends on <strong>context</strong> (whoever is in charge in one place may not be in charge in another), it runs through almost every bond —the family, the classroom, the workplace— and it is almost always <strong>unequal</strong>, even if democratic life tries to balance it. <strong>Authority</strong> is something more: not just obtaining obedience, but being recognised as someone it is fitting, or obligatory, to obey. Hence there are those who gather much power and little authority (they are feared, but not respected) and those who enjoy authority with hardly any power, like an admired person who holds no office.</p>\n<h3>Legality, legitimacy and Weber's types</h3>\n<p>A norm can be <strong>legal</strong> (the legal order recognises it), <strong>illegal</strong> (it breaks it) or <strong>a-legal</strong> (no law regulates it yet). But something being legal is not enough for it to be just: that is what <strong>legitimacy</strong> asks. A good exercise is to chain up the whys: why must I pay this tax? Because a law requires it. And why does that law bind? Because an elected parliament passed it. And why does what that parliament decides count? Because a Constitution authorises it and protects certain rights. At the end of the chain there is no longer another law, but values we accept: that is where legitimacy is decided, not in the mere official stamp.</p>\n<p>The sociologist <strong>Max Weber</strong> (1864-1920) distinguished three ways in which a command is accepted as legitimate: the <strong>traditional</strong> (one obeys because it has always been done so, as with an inherited king), the <strong>charismatic</strong> (through the personal force of a leader to whom extraordinary qualities are ascribed) and the <strong>rational-legal</strong> (one obeys rules and offices, not persons). They have different weak spots: the charismatic falters when the leader is gone, since charisma is hard to inherit; the traditional ties the present to the past; the rational-legal depersonalises power and subjects it to procedures, but it can turn into a cold machinery. In practice, regimes usually mix all three.</p>\n<p>A century before the moderns founded power on a pact, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/maquiavelo\">Niccolò Machiavelli</a></strong> (1469-1527) changed the very way of thinking about politics. In <em>The Prince</em> (1513) he does not ask how the ideal ruler <em>ought</em> to be, but how power is <strong>won and kept</strong> as it really is: he separates <strong>politics from morality</strong> and religion, and attends to what he calls the “effective truth of the matter”, how people actually act and not how they should. A ruler’s success depends on his <strong>virtù</strong> (decisiveness and skill) and on <strong>fortune</strong> (circumstances). Hence his most famous dilemma: is it better for the prince to be loved or feared? Since love depends on others and fear on oneself, he answers that, if one must choose, it is safer to be feared — though never <strong>hated</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Machiavelli: political realism and reason of State</h3><p>What makes Machiavelli the initiator of <strong>political realism</strong> is not any praise of evil, but a change of question. Before him, treatises on good government sketched the perfect prince whom we should all desire; he decides instead to look at how people and States really behave, with their ambitions and their fears, and to draw from that rules that work. This gaze has an uncomfortable consequence: politics acquires a logic <strong>of its own</strong>, which does not always coincide with that of private morality. The ruler is answerable for something no private individual has in charge—the survival of the whole community—and for this reason he may find himself obliged, for the common good, to do things we would reprove in ordinary life: dissimulate, break a promise that has become harmful, employ harshness when softness would bring a greater evil. He even distinguishes between cruelty used all at once and out of necessity, which can put an end to a conflict, and cruelty that drags on and merely sows hatred.</p><p>It is the seed of what would later be called <strong>reason of State</strong>: the preservation and security of the State as the ultimate criterion of political decision. From this comes the formula that usually summarises—and distorts—his thought, ‘the end justifies the means’. Machiavelli never wrote it in those words, but he did argue that a ruler is judged by his <strong>results</strong>, and that certain reprehensible means are excused if they genuinely save the community, never if they merely feed personal ambition. Nor is he an unlimited cold calculator: he insists that the prince must avoid being <strong>hated</strong>, respect the goods and honour of his subjects, and rely on the affection of the people rather than on that of the powerful. And alongside <strong>virtù</strong> he places <strong>fortune</strong>, that portion of chance which no skill fully controls: he compared it to a river that sweeps everything away when it floods, but against which one can raise dykes and canals in times of calm. To govern well is, for him, to prepare for what cannot be foreseen.</p><p>From this comes one of his most quoted pieces of advice: the good ruler must combine the strength of the <strong>lion</strong>, which frightens wolves, with the cunning of the <strong>fox</strong>, which knows how to recognise traps, and must not bind himself to his word when keeping it would harm him and the reasons that led him to promise have already disappeared. He adds a very modern warning: in politics, facts count as much as <strong>appearances</strong>, because most people judge by what they see and by the outcome, and few get close enough to touch what you truly are. This is not gratuitous cynicism, but the uncomfortable observation that whoever commands moves among people who are not always good, and that governing as if all of them were good leads to ruin.</p>\n<h3>The other Machiavelli: the republic and liberty</h3><p>To reduce Machiavelli to an adviser to tyrants is to keep only half of the figure. In his <em>Discourses on the First Decade of Titus Livy</em>, written almost at the same time as <em>The Prince</em>, he enthusiastically defends the <strong>republic</strong> and takes ancient Rome as his model. There he argues that, where a people is accustomed to governing itself, liberty is far preferable to the rule of one alone, and that a free city prospers more because each citizen strives knowing that what is his is protected and works for himself rather than for a master. The prince who concentrates all power would be, at most, a remedy for founding or reforming a corrupt State; the desirable destination, once order has been established, is shared liberty.</p><p>Even more surprising is his defence of <strong>conflict</strong>. Far from seeing disputes between the people and the powerful as a disease to be cured, he affirms that these tensions, so long as they are channelled through the laws and not through violence, were precisely what kept Rome free and gave rise to its best institutions. Liberty, for him, does not spring from perfect concord—almost always suspected of concealing the domination of a few—but from a vigilant balance in which no force can outweigh the others. Hence his appreciation of the <strong>mixed government</strong>, which distributes power, and of a religion and customs that sustain love of the public good. For this reason he calls for citizens with <strong>civic virtue</strong>, ready to care for the common good and not only their own advantage, and for firm laws that check corruption, his great enemy. This republican Machiavelli, who admires self-government and distrusts any power without counterweight, has nourished modern democratic thought as much as the author of <em>The Prince</em>, and reminds us that one and the same thinker may be read in opposite ways depending on which of his works one opens.</p><p>It is also worth remembering where he was writing from. Machiavelli had served the Florentine republic as secretary and diplomat; when the Medici regained power, he was removed, briefly imprisoned and forced to retire to the countryside. From that exile he wrote his works, partly to win back the favour of the new masters, partly to understand why States arise, grow and decay. That double experience—servant of a republic and witness to its fall—explains why both the adviser of princes and the lover of liberty coexist in him, and why centuries later both the defenders of strong power and the partisans of self-government claimed him alike.</p>\n<h3>Foucault: power as a relation that produces</h3><p>Much later, the French philosopher <strong><a href=\"#biografias/foucault\">Michel Foucault</a></strong> (1926-1984) proposed looking at power in a different way. We usually imagine it as something that is <strong>possessed</strong>—held by the king, the State, the boss—and which serves above all to <strong>prohibit</strong> and repress. Foucault suggests that this is only a part: power <strong>circulates</strong>, runs through all everyday relations and does not reside in a single centre. And it is, above all, <strong>productive</strong>: it does not limit itself to saying ‘no’, but shapes conduct, bodies and ways of being. He called it <strong>disciplinary</strong> power, the kind that acts in the school, the hospital, the barracks or the prison through timetables, surveillance and examinations that make us ‘normal’ almost without our noticing. To this he adds the idea of <strong>knowledge and power</strong> intertwined: each form of power generates forms of knowledge—statistics, diagnoses, files—which in turn reinforce it. And with <strong>biopower</strong> he describes how the modern State administers the life of entire populations: birth rates, health, hygiene. It is not a theory meant to replace Weber or Marx, but an invitation to trace power also where we did not look for it, in the small gestures of each day; and, if it is everywhere, it is likewise possible to resist it everywhere.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>It is much safer to be feared than loved, if one must do without one of the two. — Machiavelli, <em>The Prince</em>, chap. XVII</p> </blockquote>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: can a good ruler always be honest, or does politics sometimes force one to “get one’s hands dirty”? Where would you draw the line? You will return to this in Year 2, when studying the birth of modernity; you can find him among the thinkers of power in the <a href=\"#genealogias/pol\">“Power and the State”</a> line in Genealogies.</p>\n<p><strong>Failed states and outlaw states.</strong> Max Weber, in the lecture he entitled <em>Politics as a Vocation</em> (1919), characterised the state as that human community which manages to impose, within a given territory, its claim to be the sole holder of the legitimate use of physical force. From this idea we can distinguish two contrary paths by which a state becomes a problem.</p><ul><li>The <strong>failed state</strong> is one that does not achieve such a monopoly. It retains its symbols, and even occupies a seat in the United Nations, but lacks real control over its territory: each region falls into the hands of militias or armed groups, justice is paralysed and people find themselves defenceless. This is usually accompanied by internal conflicts, famines and populations forced to flee, and into that power vacuum move criminal networks, human traffickers or terrorist organisations seeking a hideout.</li><li>The <strong>outlaw state</strong> represents the opposite case. Internally it functions solidly and effectively, often in an authoritarian manner, and it has disciplined armed forces and police. The threat arises from its conduct abroad: attacking neighbouring countries, manufacturing prohibited weapons or funding violent organisations beyond its borders.</li></ul><p>In <em>The Law of Peoples</em> (1999), John Rawls reserves the name ‘outlaw states’ for those that refuse to submit to a reasonable order among peoples. More contested is the label ‘rogue state’, popularised by United States diplomacy during the 1990s. Its critics point out that it is used by those who have power to point at their enemies, and that it is rarely directed against their own allies.</p><p>Let us consider a case. In a certain country, after long years of internal conflict, the authorities only control the capital, while several rival militias divide up the rest. Elsewhere, a regime that admits no opposition whatsoever and possesses formidable armed forces suddenly crosses the border and occupies the neighbouring country. The threat posed by the first stems from its weakness; that of the second, from its strength.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: who is responsible for determining which state endangers the rest: a dominant power, a military coalition, the United Nations Security Council? What legitimacy justifies intervening in another country, and who watches over those who intervene?</p>\n<p>What if the power of the State were not necessary? <strong>Anarchism</strong> answers that it is not. The Russian geographer and revolutionary <strong><a href=\"#biografias/kropotkin\">Peter Kropotkin</a></strong> (1842-1921) defended a society without a State, organised from the bottom up through the <strong>free cooperation</strong> of communes and associations. Against those who saw nature as a «war of all against all», he gathered evidence that among animals and peoples <strong>mutual aid</strong> —helping one another to survive— has been as decisive as competition (<em>Mutual Aid</em>, 1902). It is not disorder: it is order <strong>without rulers</strong>, by agreement. His critics ask who then guarantees rights and curbs abuses when cooperation fails.</p>\n<h3>Anarchism: freedom without the State</h3><p>Kropotkin was not alone. <strong>Anarchism</strong> is a whole current born in the nineteenth century that shares one conviction: the State, however well-intentioned it may present itself, concentrates power in few hands and ends up creating a caste of rulers separate from everyone else. Its criticism is aimed not only at unjust governments, but at the very idea that a few should command and the rest obey; for anarchists, freedom and imposed authority are, in the long run, incompatible. The Russian revolutionary <strong>Mikhail Bakunin</strong> (1814-1876) carried that idea to its conclusion in his confrontation with Marx within the First International. Even if the workers conquered the State, he warned, that ‘workers’ State’ would give rise to a new privileged bureaucracy that would oppress in the name of the people. Time, for his followers, has not wholly proved him wrong.</p><p>That is why anarchists do not aspire to seize power, but to <strong>dissolve</strong> it. What do they put in its place? A few interlocking principles. The first is <strong>self-management</strong>: that those who carry out the work decide for themselves about their workshop, their land or their neighbourhood, without employers or officials above them. The second is <strong>federalism</strong>: instead of a central power that issues orders from above, communes and free associations coordinate from below through always revisable agreements, and rely on revocable delegates rather than permanent rulers. To this are added the <strong>mutual aid</strong> that Kropotkin studied and <strong>direct action</strong>, that is, solving problems oneself instead of waiting for an outside power to do so. Within the anarchist family there were nuances: Bakunin envisaged a <strong>collectivism</strong> in which each person would receive according to their work, whereas Kropotkin defended distributing according to each person’s <strong>needs</strong>. With all this, anarchism does not propose chaos, as the everyday use of the word suggests, but a different order, woven from below through free agreements.</p><p>Anarchism did not remain in books. At the end of the nineteenth and beginning of the twentieth centuries it was a powerful labour movement, especially in southern Europe; in Spain, the <strong>CNT</strong> trade union came to bring together hundreds of thousands of members and put experiments in collectivisation into practice. Within the movement there was much debate about means: some trusted in strikes and patient organisation, while a minority defended ‘propaganda by the deed’, attacks that often turned people against the movement. That tension between the goal—a free society without coercion—and the means to reach it runs through its whole history and connects with the second of the debates that follow.</p>\n<h3>Two open debates</h3><p><strong>Is the State necessary, or could we organise ourselves without it?</strong> On one side are those who consider it indispensable: for Hobbes, without a common power to restrain us we would return to fear of one another; for Weber, only a State that reserves for itself the legitimate use of force can ensure order and enforce rights equally. Even liberalism, which prefers a reduced State, entrusts it at least with protecting life, agreements and property, and some, like the philosopher Robert Nozick, defend a ‘minimal State’ that does no more than that. On the other side, anarchists such as Bakunin and Kropotkin reply that this protection costs too much —with a minority in command— and that free cooperation could fulfil those tasks without masters; along a similar line, Thoreau championed disobedience in the face of an unjust State. The objection that always remains standing is that of their critics: when cooperation fails, who protects the weakest and checks the one who abuses?</p><p><strong>Does politics require ‘getting one's hands dirty’?</strong> Here Machiavelli reappears. Weber gave the question its classic form by distinguishing two ethics: that of <strong>conviction</strong>, which remains faithful to its principles whatever happens, and that of <strong>responsibility</strong>, which obliges one to answer also for the <strong>consequences</strong> of what one does and of what one omits. Whoever governs, he argued, cannot ignore that sometimes securing a good requires murky means, and must bear that tension in conscience. Opposite him, Kant and his followers maintain that ends do not sanctify means: an unjust means corrupts the end it claims to serve, and there are lines that should not be crossed however good the results promised; already in the twentieth century, Gandhi insisted that means and ends must be coherent, like the seed and the tree. No closed verdict is reached here: it is an <strong>open debate</strong> that each generation raises anew, including in today's politics.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: do we need a State that commands in order to live in order, or could we organise ourselves by free agreement? In what things do we already cooperate without anyone forcing us? You will meet Kropotkin in Year 2; it is an <strong>open debate</strong>, with no closed answer.</p>\n<h3>State, nation, country and homeland: four words that do not mean the same thing</h3>\n<p>It is worth not confusing four terms that everyday speech blends together. The <strong>state</strong> is a legal reality: a sovereign power over a territory and a population, with its institutions. The <strong>country</strong> is above all a territory, with its geography and its administration. The <strong>nation</strong> is a community that feels bound together by a shared history, language or culture. And the <strong>homeland</strong> names the emotional bond with that community, the sense of belonging to a place. These are distinct planes —the legal, the territorial, the cultural and the emotional— and they need not coincide.</p>\n<p>The modern <strong>nation-state</strong> assumed that each nation should have its own state, but reality almost never fits. There are <strong>plurinational states</strong>, which bring together several nations, and there are nations divided among several states or with no state of their own. The Basque case, on both sides of the Franco-Spanish border, shows that cultural boundaries and political ones need not go together. Separating these planes helps us discuss more calmly about identity, belonging and self-government, which is an open political debate, legitimate on all sides.</p>\n<h2>4. Justice: what is a just society?</h2>\n<p><strong>Justice</strong> is the virtue of institutions: giving each person what is due to them and distributing burdens and benefits fairly. <strong>Plato</strong> already imagined in the <em>Republic</em> a just city as one in which each part performs its function in harmony. In the 20th century, <strong>John Rawls</strong> proposed a thought experiment: imagine that you design the rules of society behind a <strong>‘veil of ignorance’</strong>, without knowing what place you will occupy in it (rich or poor, healthy or ill). We would choose only rules that are just for everyone, because any position could fall to us. From this he derives two principles: equal liberties for all, and that inequalities are justified only if they benefit those who have the least.</p>\n<p><strong>Aristotle</strong> had already distinguished two types of justice. <strong>Distributive</strong> justice distributes goods and burdens among the members of the community according to some criterion; <strong>corrective</strong> justice restores the balance broken between two parties (a harm, a broken contract), and in it it does not matter who each person is. The great debate is over the criterion of distribution: the same to everyone (<strong>equality</strong>), to each according to their <strong>merit</strong> or their <strong>need</strong>; the <strong>libertarian</strong> position adds that it is enough for each person to keep what they have acquired <strong>legitimately</strong>. Choosing a criterion is choosing a model of society.</p>\n<h3>Formal justice and the criteria of distribution</h3>\n<p><strong>Transnational corporations and human rights.</strong> Human rights were conceived to limit the power of states. But today some corporations move more money than many countries and have factories, suppliers and customers all over the world. Do they also have duties?</p><p>In 2003, the UN Sub-Commission on the Promotion and Protection of Human Rights approved the <em>Norms on the Responsibilities of Transnational Corporations and Other Business Enterprises with Regard to Human Rights</em>. Among other things, they required:</p><ul><li>not to discriminate against anyone on the basis of sex, origin or religion;</li><li>to pay a wage that allows a dignified life and to respect workers’ freedom of association;</li><li>not to bribe authorities or officials;</li><li>to protect consumers and the environment.</li></ul><p>The problem is that these Norms were <strong>not binding</strong>: the UN Commission on Human Rights declared in 2004 that they had no legal force. The Guiding Principles approved in 2011 took up the idea that businesses must respect human rights, but they too do not bind like a treaty. And here the problem of failed states reappears: if a corporation works in a country whose government does not control its territory or can be bought, who demands that it comply? The weaker the state, the more everything depends on the will of the corporation itself.</p><p>Imagine a mobile phone multinational that commissions an independent firm to review its suppliers’ factories on another continent: working hours, wages, child labour, waste. And it publishes the results, including the bad ones. No law obliges it to do so, but now anyone can compare it with its competitors.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: should the consumer reward ethical companies even if their product costs more? Is it fair to place that responsibility on the buyer, or should it be required by law from the producer?</p>\n<p>There is a core of justice on which almost all of us agree: <strong>giving each their due</strong> and <strong>treating equally what is equal and differently what is different</strong>. This is called <strong>formal</strong> justice because it fixes the form —making no arbitrary exceptions— without yet saying what each one's ‘due’ is. Disagreement begins right there: on which features count for distributing.</p>\n<p>That is why we need <strong>material criteria</strong> of distribution, and each one draws a different society: in <strong>equal</strong> shares, according to each person's <strong>need</strong>, according to <strong>merit</strong> (which in turn can be measured by effort, by result or by contribution) or, in the libertarian version, simply respecting what each has legitimately acquired. It is also worth separating <strong>procedural</strong> justice (that the rules of the game be fair) from justice of <strong>outcome</strong> (that the final distribution seem acceptable to us). A case to think it over: if a club has four tickets for a match and six members who want them, does it draw lots (equality of opportunity), give them to those who have never been (need), to those who collaborate most (merit) or to those who pay first (the acquired)? To choose a criterion is already to choose a model of common life.</p>\n<p><strong>Something to think about:</strong> imagine you have to write the rules of a society —taxes, healthcare, education, work— without yet knowing what place you will occupy in it: whether you will be born rich or poor, healthy or sick, here or far away. Would your rules change from the ones you would propose knowing your place? That is the idea of the <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-velo\">veil of ignorance</a>, and it shows why human rights work as a bridge between ethics and politics: they are the minimum we would want guaranteed for anyone, including for ourselves in the worst case.</p>\n<h3>Other ways of understanding what is just</h3><p>We have already mentioned that Aristotle distinguishes distributive from corrective justice. It is worth pausing over this by turning to the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, specifically its fifth book, where the philosopher does more than separate two kinds of justice: he explains that each follows its own type of calculation. First, however, he introduces a prior distinction. The term justice (<em>dikaiosýnē</em>) serves to designate complete virtue considered in its relation to others, that is, respect for the laws of the polis. But it can also refer to a particular virtue: that of not taking for oneself more than what is due. The two kinds we are going to study belong to this second sense.</p><p><strong>Distributive justice</strong> is concerned with allocating shared goods, such as honours, offices or public funds. It is guided by a proportion that interpreters have called <strong>geometric</strong>: someone who, according to the criterion adopted, is worth twice as much as another ought to receive twice as much. Problems arise when equal persons obtain different portions, or different persons obtain identical portions. Aristotle makes a very subtle remark here (V, 3): no one disputes that distribution should be made according to merit (<em>axía</em>), but each person understands merit in a different way. For democrats it consists in being free; for oligarchs, in being wealthy; for aristocrats, in being virtuous. Imagine that four classmates submit a piece of work and receive an 8. Does that 8 belong to everyone equally, or should it be distributed according to each member's contribution? And if we opt for the latter, what do we consider contribution: the time devoted, the intellectual input or having got up to present in front of the class? The rule of proportion raises no doubts; the real debate turns on what is taken as the measure.</p><p>The workings of <strong>corrective justice</strong> are very different. It applies to relations between two parties, whether freely entered into (a sale, a loan) or involuntary (a theft, an assault). Here the standing of each person is irrelevant: the judge treats them as equals and attends only to the harm caused. Hence its calculation is <strong>arithmetic</strong>: what is in excess is taken from the one who has gained and restored to the one who has lost, until the central equilibrium is recovered. If you lend a friend from Getxo 20 euros to go to a concert, she will have to return those 20 euros, even if her marks are better than yours or you receive more money from your parents. Centuries later, Thomas Aquinas gave the name <strong>commutative</strong> to the justice proper to exchanges, and that is the term habitually used in textbooks.</p><p>To complete the picture, Aristotle introduces <em>epieíkeia</em> or equity. Since the law is formulated in universal terms, it may happen that it does not fit a particular situation well. Rectifying it in that situation, as the legislator would have done had he taken it into account, does not mean abandoning justice, but perfecting it.</p>\n<h3>Distributing things or expanding possibilities?</h3><p>Almost all the theories we have seen discuss how much each person should receive: money, resources, primary goods. The Indian economist and philosopher <strong>Amartya Sen</strong> (<em>Development as Freedom</em>, 1999) asks something else: what each person can do and be with what they receive. One example: we give the same bicycle to two people. For one it is freedom, because they will be able to go to school, to the hills or to see their friends. The other cannot pedal because of an injury, and for them the bicycle is an object in the storage room. The good is identical, but the real freedom it provides is very different. Sen calls <strong>functionings</strong> what a person manages to do or be (being well nourished, reading, participating in the life of the community) and <strong>capabilities</strong> the set of functionings that are genuinely within their reach. For him, a country's development is not measured only by income, but by how much those real freedoms are expanded.</p><p>The American philosopher <strong>Martha Nussbaum</strong> (<em>Creating Capabilities</em>, 2011) takes a step that Sen avoided: she proposes a list of ten central capabilities that every decent society should guarantee above a threshold. Among them are life, health, bodily integrity, imagination and thought, emotions, practical reason, affiliation with others and, curiously, play. Her critics object that such a list can become paternalistic: who decides which capabilities count for everyone? Nussbaum replies that she guarantees capabilities, she does not force anyone to exercise them. Someone who has the freedom to play may choose not to play.</p><h3>Each good, its own rule</h3><p><strong>Michael Walzer</strong> (<em>Spheres of Justice</em>, 1983) questions the idea that there is a single criterion for distribution. Each good has a social meaning and, with it, its own rule. Healthcare is distributed according to need; qualifications, according to demonstrated merits; public offices, through the vote; love, by free choice. Injustice appears when one good invades the sphere of another, when money buys votes, court decisions or school places. Walzer calls this situation <strong>complex equality</strong>: there can be inequalities within each sphere, as long as none dominates the others. His critics ask who fixes the meaning of each good and whether, by relying on what each community shares, he is left without tools to criticise an unjust community. His defenders reply that an abstract criterion, valid for everything, ignores how we actually live.</p>\n<h3>Redistribution or recognition</h3><p>So far we have talked about distributing: goods, opportunities, capabilities. But some injustices cannot be fixed with money. Imagine someone who earns the same as their colleagues, but who is ridiculed for their accent, their religion or their sexual orientation. Or think of a language that is always treated as second-rate. The American philosopher <strong>Nancy Fraser</strong> summarised this in a pair of concepts. There are injustices of <strong>distribution</strong>, which have to do with the economy: exploitation, poverty, starvation wages. And there are injustices of <strong>recognition</strong>, which have to do with culture: contempt, stereotypes, invisibility. The first call for redistribution; the second, for revaluing those who have been disparaged. Later Fraser added a third dimension, representation: who has a voice in decisions.</p><p><strong>Iris Marion Young</strong> (<em>Justice and the Politics of Difference</em>, 1990) criticised what she called the “distributive paradigm”, which reduces justice to distributing things as if they were slices of a cake. For her, injustice is above all domination and oppression, and oppression has five faces: exploitation, marginalisation, powerlessness, cultural imperialism (when the dominant group imposes its way of seeing as if it were the only one) and violence. Several of these cannot be cured by distributing better, but by changing institutions, norms and ways of looking.</p><p>At this point an <strong>open debate</strong> begins: can the two kinds of injustice be separated? Fraser argues that it is useful to distinguish them in order to think clearly, even though in real life they are mixed. Gender, for example, involves at once worse-paid work and cultural contempt. Other authors, such as the German philosopher Axel Honneth, maintain that at bottom everything is recognition: an unjust wage also expresses that someone's work is not valued. From another side come two criticisms. Some left-wing thinkers fear that talking so much about identities diverts attention from economic inequality, which affects all groups. Some liberals fear the opposite: that treating people according to their group clashes with equality before the law and locks each person into a label. Those who defend recognition reply that neutrality often hides the norm of the majority group.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: think of an injustice you know at first hand. Would it be fixed with more money, with more respect, or with both? Can someone be treated justly in the distribution and yet not feel treated as an equal?</p>\n<h2>5. Forms of government and democracy</h2>\n<p>Since the Greeks, governments have been classified according to <strong>who rules</strong>: one (monarchy), a few (aristocracy) or many (Aristotle called it <em>politeia</em>, or polity), each with its degenerate form when it governs in its own interest (tyranny, oligarchy and, for Aristotle, democracy). Modern <strong>democracy</strong>—‘government of the people’—is based on popular sovereignty, participation, equality before the law and respect for minorities. It is not just voting: it requires freedoms, pluralism, separation of powers and control of power. Its greatest enemies are <strong>manipulation</strong> (hoaxes and propaganda), extreme inequality and apathy. That is why political philosophy insists: democracy is looked after by thinking critically, exactly what this course trains.</p>\n<h3>Forms of government and forms of democracy</h3><p>The classification of governments you already know has a precise place: Aristotle sets it out in the <em>Politics</em> (Book III, ch. 7). It combines two questions. The first is <strong>how many rule</strong>: one, few or many. The second, more important, is <strong>for whom they govern</strong>. If they seek the common good, the form is right: monarchy, aristocracy and <em>politeía</em>. If they seek the advantage of those who rule, it deviates: tyranny, oligarchy and democracy. Note that, for him, “democracy” names a deviation: the government of the many, who are usually poor, in their own interest. In the next chapter he specifies that what truly separates oligarchy from democracy is not so much number as wealth: the rich rule or the poor rule. That is why his <em>politeía</em> mixes features of both and rests on a broad middle class.</p><p>Today we use “democracy” as a term of praise and distinguish above all two ways of practising it. In <strong>direct democracy</strong>, citizens decide the laws themselves. In <strong>representative democracy</strong>, they elect those who decide in their name and can hold them to account. Classical Athens was quite close to the first: adult male citizens met in the <em>ekklesía</em> to vote on laws, wars and treaties. Even more striking was <strong>sortition</strong>. The members of the Council of Five Hundred, the juries in the courts and most magistrates were not elected but chosen by lot, sometimes using a stone machine, the <em>klerotérion</em>. Only a few offices requiring expertise, such as generals, were elected. The underlying idea was equality: any citizen could govern and be governed in turn, and no one could buy office through a campaign. Aristotle came to see sortition as a democratic feature and election as an aristocratic or oligarchic one. Today it seems to us exactly the opposite.</p><p>No democracy today is entirely direct, but some mix the two models. The most cited case is <strong>Switzerland</strong>. Any reform of the federal Constitution must be approved by referendum, and it requires a double majority: of the people and of the cantons. With 50,000 signatures, a law already passed by Parliament can be put to a vote, and with 100,000 a constitutional reform can be proposed by popular initiative. The Swiss vote in this way several times a year, on matters as different as pensions or the purchase of military aircraft. In two cantons, Glarus and Appenzell Innerrhoden, the open-air assembly, the <em>Landsgemeinde</em>, still decides by show of hands. Would something like that work in a city of millions of inhabitants, or in a school?</p>\n<h3>“The worst form of government, except all the others”</h3><p>Few sentences about democracy are repeated as often as this one. Winston Churchill, then leader of the opposition, said it in a debate on a bill that curtailed the power of the House of Lords to delay legislation:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>“Many forms of Government have been tried, and will be tried in this world of sin and woe. No one pretends that democracy is perfect or all-wise. Indeed, it has been said that democracy is the worst form of Government except all those other forms that have been tried from time to time.” — Winston Churchill, speech in the House of Commons, 11 November 1947</p></blockquote><p>Note one detail: Churchill says “it has been said”. He does not present the idea as his own, although it has passed into history under his name. More interesting than its authorship is the type of argument. It is not a positive defence, of the kind “democracy is good because it achieves justice”, but <strong>comparative and negative</strong>: democracy may work badly, but the known alternatives work worse.</p><p>Why is it a strong defence? First, because it asks very little. It does not require believing that the people are wise or that majorities get it right, and so it also convinces those who distrust ordinary people. Second, because it rests on experience: absolute monarchies, military dictatorships and single-party regimes have had their chance, and the record is plain to see. Third, because it points to a modest but decisive virtue: democracy allows <strong>errors to be corrected</strong>. Popper formulated it by changing the question. What matters is not who should govern, but how to rid ourselves of bad rulers without bloodshed. And the economist Amartya Sen argued that no democracy with elections and a free press has suffered a major famine, because no government can ignore such a thing without paying for it at the ballot box.</p><p>What does it leave out? Several things. It does not say <em>which</em> democracy is the least bad: that of Switzerland, that of Athens, the one we know here? It looks only backwards, at what has been “tried”, and is no use for judging new proposals. Moreover, it reduces democracy to a lesser evil and forgets the reasons why many defend it for its own sake: that we treat others as equals when each voice counts the same, or that a person is free only if they participate in the laws that bind them. Some fear that a democracy defended only as “the least bad” will be easily abandoned when someone promises something better. Others reply that precisely that modesty protects it from fanaticism.</p>\n<p>In 1776 Enlightenment political thought moved from theory to fact. <strong><a href=\"#biografias/jefferson\">Thomas Jefferson</a></strong> (1743–1826) was the author of the American <strong>Declaration of Independence</strong>, which states that all people are born equal and possess rights that no one can take away from them —‘life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness’ (Declaration of Independence, 4 July 1776; our translation)— and that the reason for the existence of governments is to secure them, based on the <strong>consent of the governed</strong>. This is the principle of <strong>self-government</strong>: legitimate authority comes from the people, not from a monarch or from divinity. Nevertheless, Jefferson owned slaves, an inconsistency that democracy needed centuries to face.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: if a text proclaims that «we are all equal» but the one who writes it does not live up to it, does the idea lose its value, or does it hold as a goal still to be won? You will meet Jefferson in Year 2.</p>\n<h3>What distinguishes a liberal democracy</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Pluralism:</strong> many parties and opinions compete, as opposed to the single party.</li><li><strong>Rule of law:</strong> whoever governs is also subject to it, as opposed to arbitrary power.</li><li><strong>Accountability:</strong> whoever rules answers for what they do, as opposed to impunity.</li><li><strong>Free and regular elections,</strong> as opposed to office held for life or inherited.</li><li><strong>An active civil society</strong> (press, associations, trade unions), as opposed to state control of public life.</li><li><strong>Majority rule with minority rights:</strong> the winner governs, but without crushing the loser.</li><li><strong>Representation:</strong> citizens choose those who decide and can replace them.</li></ul>\n<h2>6. Human rights and the rule of law</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-31\"><strong>Human rights</strong> are the minimum demands that belong to every person simply by being a person, without distinction. Set out in the 1948 <strong>Universal Declaration</strong>, they work as a limit that no power may cross. A <strong>rule-of-law State</strong> is one in which the government too is subject to the law and those rights are guaranteed. Opposed to it, <strong>totalitarianism</strong>—analysed by <strong>Hannah Arendt</strong>—is the form of power that annuls freedom, plurality and public life.</p>\n<p>Human rights have a <strong>prehistory</strong>. The ancient world knew universalisms that nonetheless left out the majority: neither Athenian democracy nor Roman citizenship reached slaves, foreigners or women. Against this, the <strong>Stoics</strong> held that all human beings are citizens of one and the same world, an idea the Stoic Hierocles drew as concentric circles that it is good to draw gradually towards the centre. In the modern age, Locke spoke of <strong>natural rights</strong> prior to any state, and the eighteenth century put them in writing: the Virginia Declaration (1776), the American and French declarations, and that of Olympe de Gouges for women. The decisive step came in <strong>1945</strong>, with the creation of the UN after two world wars, the Holocaust and the end of the colonial empires: only then was the urgency felt to proclaim rights for the whole species.</p>\n<p>Human rights are usually arranged in <strong>three generations</strong>, linked to the motto of the French Revolution: <strong>civil and political</strong> rights (liberty: expression, voting, guarantees against power); <strong>economic, social and cultural</strong> rights (equality: education, health care, decent work), which require the state to act and not merely to refrain; and those of <strong>solidarity</strong> (fraternity: peace, a healthy environment, the development of peoples). Their philosophical foundation is disputed: <strong>Bentham</strong>, for example, called natural rights ‘nonsense’, because for him only what a law backs is a right.</p>\n<h3>The generations of human rights</h3>\n<p>Classifying human rights into ‘generations’ has an author: the jurist <strong>Karel Vasak</strong>, a Czech refugee in France and an official of the Council of Europe and UNESCO, proposed it in 1977, taking as his guide the motto of the French Revolution, <em>liberté, égalité, fraternité</em>. Each word points to a type of duty. <strong>Civil and political</strong> rights (<strong>first generation</strong>, liberty) draw a line that power <strong>must not cross</strong>: that nobody reads your messages without a court order, that you are not punished for criticising the Government, that you can vote. <strong>Economic, social and cultural</strong> rights (<strong>second</strong>, equality) demand <strong>services</strong>: state schools, a family doctor, benefits if you lose your job. Those of the <strong>third</strong> (fraternity or solidarity) belong to <strong>peoples</strong> and to humanity rather than to each person, and no State can guarantee them on its own: a healthy environment (think of a clean river that crosses several countries), peace and development.</p>\n<h3>Generations of rights: an underlying tension</h3><p>You already know the classification proposed by Karel Vašák in 1977: liberty, equality and solidarity. This classification hides a tension that is worth examining closely. <strong>First-generation</strong> rights are usually described as <em>limits</em> on power, because they ask the State to refrain: not to censor, not to detain without trial, not to enter your home. <strong>Second-generation</strong> rights, by contrast, ask for <em>provision</em>, because they require it to act: to build schools, to pay doctors, to maintain a pension. Some say ‘do not touch me’ and the others, ‘take responsibility’.</p><p>Two positions arise from this difference. Some argue that only the former are rights in the strict sense. They cost little and can be demanded before a judge from day one. Moreover, they do not depend on money being available: a poor State can stop torturing, but it cannot open a thousand hospitals tomorrow. The International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights of 1966 itself admits this difference. It asks States to make <em>progressive</em> advances according to their resources, whereas the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights requires them to be respected immediately. During the Cold War, moreover, each bloc defended its own list. The Western bloc put freedoms first and the Soviet bloc, social provisions. That is why there was not a single covenant, but two.</p><p>Others reply that the distinction is weaker than it seems. Stephen Holmes and Cass Sunstein (<em>The Cost of Rights</em>, 1999) reminded us that liberty also costs money. A fair trial requires courts, legal aid lawyers and police. The right to vote requires censuses, ballot boxes and vote counts. And they add that rights without each other are lame: what use is press freedom to someone who cannot read? That is why the Vienna Conference of 1993 declared that human rights are <strong>indivisible and interdependent</strong>: there is no first-class list and second-class list.</p><p>A close example helps to see this. That no one forbids you to study Bachillerato is a right of the first family. That there is a public secondary school near home, with paid teaching staff, belongs to the second. If the latter is lacking, the former remains on paper. But if the State provides everything, will it not end up deciding everything? In that double suspicion lies the tension.</p>\n<h3>A European story told as if it were everyone’s?</h3><p>The word <em>generation</em> suggests an orderly succession: first come freedoms, then social rights and finally solidarity. Some critics see in this a <strong>Eurocentric</strong> narrative, that is, the history of France and the United Kingdom presented as if it were that of humanity. Their reasons carry weight. Outside Europe the order was often different. The Mexican Constitution of 1917 included labour rights before many European constitutions. For colonised peoples, moreover, the first right to be won was that of deciding their own destiny, and that right appears in the ‘third’ generation. The African Charter on Human and Peoples’ Rights (1981) links individual rights, peoples’ rights and duties towards the community. That combination does not fit well into Vašák’s ladder.</p><p>Those who defend the classification respond in two ways. First, Vašák did not intend to write universal history, but to offer a didactic tool for ordering types of duties. Its value lies in that clarity, not in chronology. Second, that an idea was born in one place does not make it the property of that place, just as algebra is not only Arab nor democracy only Greek. In the committee that drafted the 1948 Declaration worked, among others, the Chinese Peng Chun Chang and the Lebanese Charles Malik, who contributed traditions different from the European one.</p><p>The debate remains open, and so does the list. As you have already seen, a <strong>fourth generation</strong> of digital rights is being discussed. To privacy and access to the internet is added the idea of one’s own <em>digital identity</em>, which nobody should be able to impersonate or manipulate. A real case shows that this is already happening. In 2014, the Court of Justice of the European Union recognised that a person can ask a search engine to stop displaying old data that are no longer relevant. This is what has been called the ‘right to be forgotten’.</p><p>Alongside them appear the so-called <strong>emerging rights</strong>. These are demands that are gaining recognition without yet being fully consolidated. In 2010, the UN General Assembly recognised the right to safe drinking water and sanitation. Some celebrate this expansion as a sign that rights are alive. Others warn that, if everything becomes a human right, the expression loses force and basic rights become diluted.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Value</th><th>Examples</th><th>Articles of the 1948 Declaration</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>First</strong></td><td>Liberty</td><td>Life, freedom of expression, voting, a fair trial</td><td>Arts 3-21</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Second</strong></td><td>Equality</td><td>Work, social security, health, education and culture</td><td>Arts 22-27</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Third</strong></td><td>Solidarity</td><td>Peace, development, environment, common heritage</td><td>The Declaration barely touches on them (art. 28); they are developed in later conventions</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>And people are already talking of a <strong>fourth generation</strong>: <strong>digital</strong> rights linked to new technologies, such as the protection of personal data, access to the internet or not being discriminated against by an algorithm. It is not a closed category, nor one accepted by everyone: while some claim it as a generation in its own right—Spain approved a Charter of Digital Rights in 2021—others reply that we should not invent new rights, but apply existing ones to new problems. It is an <strong>open debate</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Do animals have rights?</h3><p>So far we have spoken of <em>human</em> rights. But what if the circle should be wider? The question has a famous precedent in Jeremy Bentham. In a note to his <em>An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation</em> (1789) he compares the treatment of animals with the treatment of slaves and shifts the moral criterion:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>The question is not, Can they reason?, nor, Can they talk? but, Can they suffer? — Jeremy Bentham, <em>An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation</em>, ch. XVII, note (own translation)</p></blockquote><p>Peter Singer took up this idea in <em>Animal Liberation</em> (1975). He argued that giving less weight to the pain of a pig than to that of a person, merely because of species, is a prejudice similar to racism. He called it <strong>speciesism</strong>, a term that Richard Ryder had coined shortly before. Note a nuance: Singer is a utilitarian and speaks not so much of rights as of <em>equal consideration of interests</em>. It was Tom Regan who expressly defended <em>rights</em> for animals (<em>The Case for Animal Rights</em>, 1983). For Regan, many animals are ‘subjects of a life’ with their own value. In 1993, Singer and Paola Cavalieri promoted the <strong>Great Ape Project</strong>, which demands for chimpanzees, gorillas and orangutans three guarantees: not to be killed, not to be deprived of liberty without cause and not to be tortured.</p><p>The classic objection says that rights go together with duties. Only someone who can understand norms, answer for their actions and respect the rights of others would belong to the moral community that recognises rights. A wolf cannot respect the right of a sheep. Authors such as Roger Scruton (<em>Animal Rights and Wrongs</em>, 1996) maintain that we have duties <em>towards</em> animals, such as not being cruel or mistreating them, without their being holders of rights. Kant had already defended something similar: cruelty to animals is wrong because it brutalises the person who practises it.</p><p>Defenders respond with the so-called argument from ‘marginal cases’. A baby or a person with a severe intellectual disability also cannot assume duties, and no one denies them rights. Therefore, being able to fulfil duties cannot be the condition for having them. The critics’ reply is that those people belong to a species whose normal form of life does include moral responsibility.</p><p>Think about everyday life: a factory farm, a zoo, a medical experiment on mice that can save lives. Where would you draw the line, and with what criterion? It is an open debate.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: should ‘access to the internet’ be a human right, on a level with freedom of expression, or is it enough to regulate it through ordinary laws?</p>\n\n<p><strong>Freedom of speech</strong> and <strong>tolerance</strong> were not always taken for granted: they had to be won. Their great defender was <strong><a href=\"#biografias/voltaire\">Voltaire</a></strong> (1694-1778), who fought <strong>fanaticism</strong> and religious intolerance; for example, he defended the memory of Jean Calas, a Protestant unjustly executed (<em>Treatise on Tolerance</em>, 1763). He is summed up in a phrase that, though not literally his, captures his spirit: «I do not share what you say, but I will defend your right to say it». To tolerate is not to agree with everyone: it is to refrain from imposing by force what can only convince with arguments.</p>\n<h3>Rights in recent history: from proclamation to the courts</h3><p>A list of rights does not change the world on its own. Someone must invoke it and someone must enforce it. Two twentieth-century processes show how words were turned into deeds.</p><p>The first is <strong>Decolonisation</strong>. In 1945, much of Africa and Asia remained under European empires. On 14 December 1960, the UN General Assembly adopted resolution 1514, the <em>Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples</em>. It affirms that subjecting a people to foreign domination denies fundamental human rights and that all peoples have the right to self-determination. Six years later, that right opened Article 1 of the two 1966 covenants. It was a fruitful paradox: the colonised used the language of rights, born partly in the metropolises, against the metropolises themselves. How far that right extends today beyond the colonial case is debated. For some, it should protect any people that wishes to decide its own future. For others, it is limited to decolonisation and must respect the integrity of states. It is an open debate.</p><p>The second process is the <strong>judicial route</strong>. In 1950, the European Convention on Human Rights was signed in Rome, and to monitor compliance the <strong>European Court of Human Rights</strong> was created, based in Strasbourg. Since 1998, any person may lodge an application directly against a signatory state once domestic remedies have been exhausted. Its case law has gradually made concrete rights that were general in the text. Two cases illustrate this:</p><ul><li><em>Tyrer v. the United Kingdom</em> (1978). A teenager from the Isle of Man had been sentenced to birching. The Court held that this punishment was degrading treatment. It added that the Convention is a “living instrument” which must be read in the light of the conditions of each age.</li><li><em>Dudgeon v. the United Kingdom</em> (1981). The Court declared that punishing homosexual relations between adults in Northern Ireland as an offence violated the right to respect for private life.</li></ul><p>This way of working also has its critics. Some applaud judges for protecting minorities who would never win a vote. Others fear that an international court decides questions that elected parliaments should resolve. Who should have the last word on what a right requires: the majority or the judges?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: should we also tolerate those who preach intolerance, or would tolerating them end up destroying tolerance itself? You will meet Voltaire in Year 2, in the Enlightenment project from which these rights are born.</p>\n<p>His fellow of the same generation <strong><a href=\"#biografias/diderot\">Denis Diderot</a></strong> (1713-1784) waged the same battle with another weapon: <strong>knowledge</strong>. He directed the <em>Encyclopédie</em> (1751-1772), an immense work that gathered the knowledge of its time to put it <strong>within everyone’s reach</strong> and free it from the control of authority. Spreading knowledge was, for the Enlightenment thinkers, a way to <strong>emancipate</strong>: whoever understands the world is harder to deceive and to subdue. That is why the Encyclopédie was censored and persecuted.</p>\n<h3>Are there rights without duties?</h3><p>We usually speak of rights as something we <em>have</em> and can claim. But every right points to someone. If I have a right to education, someone has the duty to teach me. If I have a right to privacy, others have the duty not to spy on me. In this sense, rights and duties are <strong>correlative</strong>, like the two sides of a coin. The interesting question is another one: which side comes first?</p><p>The French philosopher <a href=\"#biografias/weil\">Simone Weil</a> (1909–1943) gave a counter-current answer. Shortly before her death she wrote in London <em>The Need for Roots</em> (1943, published in 1949), which opens as follows:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>The notion of obligation comes before that of rights, which is subordinate and relative to it. — Simone Weil, <em>The Need for Roots</em>, Part One, “The Needs of the Soul”, opening (own translation)</p></blockquote><p>Her reasoning is simple. A right is worth nothing if no one feels obliged to respect it. By contrast, a human being alone in the universe would have no rights against anyone, but would still have obligations. That is why Weil reproaches the revolutionaries of 1789 for having begun with rights: they invite people to claim, when what sustains living together is attention to the needs of the other. Along the same lines, the 1948 Declaration recalls in Article 29 that everyone has duties to the community. The African Charter of 1981 devotes several articles to the duties of the individual.</p><p>The rights tradition responds with strong arguments. Since Locke, rights have been understood as something the person possesses in herself and which sets a limit to power. If we make them dependent on duties, power could say to us: “first comply, and then we shall talk”. Joel Feinberg (<em>The Nature and Value of Rights</em>, 1970) imagined a town where everyone was kind out of duty, but no one could <em>claim</em> anything. Those people would lack something essential: the dignity of demanding what is theirs with their heads held high, instead of thanking for it as a favour. Moreover, they remind us that a baby has rights even though it does not yet have duties.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: when you say “I have the right to…”, do you also think about who has the duty to make it possible? Would a society be better if everyone asked first “what do I owe?” rather than “what am I owed?”, or would it run the risk of forgetting those who cannot claim? It is an open debate.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: is free access to knowledge still a tool against power today, now that information abounds but so does disinformation? You will meet Diderot in Year 2, alongside Voltaire in the Enlightenment.</p>\n<h3>Inside the Universal Declaration of Human Rights</h3>\n<p>After the Second World War, in 1948, the United Nations General Assembly adopted the <strong>Universal Declaration of Human Rights</strong>. The starting point of its <strong>preamble</strong> is the dignity proper to each person. According to Article 1, all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights; Article 2 prohibits any form of discrimination. Between Article 3 and Article 21 civil and political rights are listed (to life, to liberty, to due process, to express oneself, to participate), while Articles 22 to 27 deal with economic, social and cultural rights (to work, to receive education, to look after one’s health, to take part in culture).</p>\n<p>It matters to know what <strong>force</strong> it has. The Declaration is a <strong>resolution</strong> of the Assembly, not a <strong>treaty</strong>: it sets a common ideal, but on its own it does not legally bind states. That is why in 1966 two <strong>covenants</strong> were adopted —one on civil and political rights, the other on economic, social and cultural rights— which are indeed treaties and bind those who ratify them. In short: the Declaration inspires and the covenants bind.</p>\n<h3>Universal or relative? The debate on the foundation of rights</h3>\n<p>Do human rights hold for all cultures, or are they an imposed Western view? The <strong>Cairo Declaration</strong> (1990) proposed reading them in the light of Islamic law, and the <strong>Bangkok Declaration</strong> (1993) stressed so-called Asian values and the sovereignty of each state against a common list. That same year, the <strong>Vienna Conference</strong> reaffirmed that rights are universal, even if they must be applied with attention to each context. Beneath this lies a philosophical question: on what are rights <strong>founded</strong> —on human nature, on an agreement, on dignity— and can anything bind everyone without a shared foundation? It is an open debate that connects with the moral relativism of Topic 5.</p>\n<h2>7. Ideologies and today’s challenges</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-43\">An <strong>ideology</strong> is a set of ideas about how society should be organised. <strong>Liberalism</strong> stresses individual freedom and limits on power; <strong>socialism</strong>, equality and social justice; there are many intermediate positions. Today political philosophy faces new problems: <strong>globalisation</strong> and inequalities between countries, the <strong>ecological crisis</strong> (do we have duties to future generations?), <strong>populism</strong> and disinformation, or the power of the big technology companies. In all of them the old question reappears: how do we want to live together?</p>\n<h3>The ideological spectrum: where left and right come from</h3><p>We speak of ‘left’ and ‘right’ as if they were words that had always existed, but they have a date and place of birth. In 1789, in the National Assembly of revolutionary France, there was a debate about whether the king should keep the right to veto laws. Those who defended it sat to the right of the president of the chamber, and those who wanted to limit royal power sat to the left. That physical seating arrangement became a map: on the right, those who distrust sudden change and value inherited order; on the left, those who want to transform society in the name of equality. It is a useful map, but it simplifies: many ideologies do not fit on a single line.</p><p>The first major response to the Revolution was <strong>conservatism</strong>. The Irish politician Edmund Burke, in his <em>Reflections on the Revolution in France</em> (1790), criticised the revolutionaries for wanting to rebuild society from scratch, following only blueprints of abstract reason. For him, society is a pact between generations: it unites the living with the dead and with those yet to be born. Inherited institutions hold an accumulated wisdom that no individual, however clever, can reconstruct in an afternoon. That is why Burke does not reject all reform, but calls for reforming prudently, preserving what works.</p><p>At the other extreme, <strong>Socialism</strong> emerged, as a reaction to the misery of the working class during the Industrial Revolution. Its first authors were later called ‘utopians’. <a href=\"#biografias/owen\">Robert Owen</a>, owner of cotton factories in New Lanark (Scotland), reduced child labour, opened schools for his workers’ children and later founded a cooperative community in the United States, New Harmony. <a href=\"#biografias/fourier\">Charles Fourier</a> imagined phalansteries, communities where work would be distributed according to each person’s tastes. <a href=\"#biografias/saint-simon\">Henri de Saint-Simon</a> dreamed of a society led by scientists and industrialists instead of idle nobles. Engels labelled them utopian compared with his own socialism, which he called ‘scientific’ because it claimed to be based on the analysis of history and economics, and not on good wishes.</p><p>At the end of the nineteenth century, part of the labour movement abandoned the idea of an imminent revolution. This is <strong>social democracy</strong>: achieving improvements through elections, trade unions and Parliament, such as the eight-hour working day, pensions or public health care. From the revolutionary side it was accused of settling for merely softening capitalism; its defenders reply that it genuinely improved the lives of millions of people without paying the price of a revolution. Think of a nearby example: when a party defends raising taxes to fund Osakidetza or lowering them to leave more money in people’s pockets, it is moving on that map born in 1789.</p>\n<h3>Adam Smith in depth: the moralist who wrote about economics</h3><p>Smith is usually reduced to a phrase about self-interest, but before being an economist he was a professor of moral philosophy at Glasgow. His first book, <em>The Theory of Moral Sentiments</em> (1759), begins precisely with the opposite of selfishness:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>However selfish man may be supposed, there are evidently some principles in his nature which make him interested in the fortune of others. — Adam Smith, <em>The Theory of Moral Sentiments</em>, Part I, Section I, Chapter 1</p></blockquote><p>That principle is <strong>sympathy</strong>: the capacity to put ourselves in another’s place and feel, even if only slightly, what they feel. And to judge our own actions, Smith invents a figure: the <strong>impartial spectator</strong>, a kind of inner witness who watches what we do as someone with no interests at stake would watch it. When you are about to humiliate someone in a WhatsApp group and something tells you that ‘that is not right’, according to Smith that spectator is speaking.</p><p>In <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> (1776) appears the most famous example of the <strong>division of labour</strong>: a pin factory. A single worker, without training, might not make even twenty pins a day; but ten workers who divide the tasks (one draws the wire, another cuts it, another sharpens the point…) manage to make about forty-eight thousand. Dividing labour multiplies wealth. The famous ‘invisible hand’, by contrast, appears only once in the whole work (Book IV, Chapter 2), when discussing someone who invests in his own country seeking his gain and ends up favouring, without intending it, the general interest. To turn it into the summary of all Smith is an exaggeration.</p><p>Moreover, Smith sets limits to the market. He warned that the division of labour, if someone repeats a few simple operations all his life, can brutalise him, and for that reason he called for the state to support cheap schools so that ordinary people could learn to read, write and count (Book V, Chapter 1). And he distrusted businessmen when they came to agreements among themselves:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>People of the same trade seldom meet together, even for merriment and diversion, but the conversation ends in a conspiracy against the public, or in some contrivance to raise prices. — Adam Smith, <em>The Wealth of Nations</em>, Book I, Chapter 10, Part II</p></blockquote><p>His defence of the market was, above all, a defence of competition against privileges and monopolies. Today some read Smith as the father of the free market and others emphasise his warnings; both find support in his texts.</p>\n<h3>Classical liberalism and social liberalism</h3><p>Liberalism is not a single doctrine, but a family with internal discussions. Its <strong>classical</strong> version insists on limiting power: the state should protect life, liberty and property, guarantee contracts and, for the rest, leave people alone. John Stuart Mill gave this idea its most precise formulation in <em>On Liberty</em> (1859), with the so-called <strong>harm principle</strong>:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>The only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilised community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others. — John Stuart Mill, <em>On Liberty</em>, Chapter I</p></blockquote><p>According to this principle, the state can forbid you to drive drunk, because you endanger others, but not force you to lead a healthy life if it only affects you. However, Mill himself gradually turned towards social concerns: he separated the laws of production, which depend on nature, from the distribution of wealth, which depends on human decisions and can be changed, and looked with sympathy on workers’ cooperatives.</p><p>At the end of the nineteenth century, so-called <strong>social liberalism</strong> went a step further: it is of little use for the law to allow you to choose a profession if you are illiterate, sick or have nothing to eat. To be truly free, conditions are needed: school, health care, a minimum of security. The great crisis of 1929 reinforced this idea. The British economist John Maynard Keynes, in his <em>General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money</em> (1936), argued that the market does not always correct itself: in a crisis, if businesses and families stop spending at the same time out of fear, unemployment can remain for a long time. Then the state must intervene, spending and investing to revive demand. Keynes did not want to end capitalism, but to save it from its own traffic jams.</p><p>Here an open debate arises. For some, freedom consists in no one interfering in your life, and when the state collects taxes to give grants or subsidies, it reduces the freedom of those who pay; moreover, permanent aid can create dependence. For others, freedom without means is only a word: someone who cannot pay the bus to go to a job interview has the door open, but cannot cross it. The former fear a state that, with good intentions, ends up deciding too much for us; the latter, a market that leaves many out of the game. Both sides call themselves defenders of freedom, and what they discuss is what it means to have it.</p>\n<p>In ‘Two Concepts of Liberty’ (1958), <strong>Isaiah Berlin</strong> showed that the word contains two questions, and that many ideological disputes arise from mixing them up. How far can I act without anyone stopping me? <strong>Negative liberty</strong> answers that: the area free from interference. Who decides about my life? <strong>Positive liberty</strong> answers that: being one’s own master, being guided by one’s own reason and taking part in shared decisions. A girl from Barakaldo whom no law forbids to study Medicine, but whose family cannot pay her tuition fees: is she free? Some will say yes, and that it is enough not to put obstacles in her way; others, that without the means she does not govern her own life, and they will call for grants. Berlin warned of a danger in the second: whoever claims to know my ‘true self’ better than I do can impose things on me and call that setting me free. It is a <strong>political</strong> question, distinct from the metaphysical problem of <strong>free will</strong>.</p>\n<p>Liberalism is not only political: it also has an <strong>economic</strong> side, and its great theorist is <strong><a href=\"#biografias/smith\">Adam Smith</a></strong> (1723-1790). In <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> (1776) he argued that, when each person pursues their own interest in a <strong>free market</strong>, an “<strong>invisible hand</strong>” makes the outcome benefit the whole of society without anyone intending it; and that the <strong>division of labour</strong> multiplies wealth. It is not praise of selfishness: Smith, who had earlier written on <strong>sympathy</strong> as the basis of morality, relies on each trade, by competing, serving others in order to prosper.</p>\n<h3>Neoliberalism: Hayek and Friedman</h3><p>After the Second World War, most Western countries followed Keynes's line: welfare state, public enterprises, heavy regulation. A group of thinkers saw this as a danger, and their ideas, minority for decades, inspired from the late 1970s the governments of Margaret Thatcher in the United Kingdom and Ronald Reagan in the United States. This current is called <strong>neoliberalism</strong>, although few of its defenders use the word.</p><p>The Austrian Friedrich Hayek published <em>The Road to Serfdom</em> in 1944. His thesis: when the State tries to plan the whole economy, it needs to decide what is produced, how much and for whom. But people do not agree on what is most important, so someone has to impose their scale of values on others. Thus, little by little, economic planning requires concentrating power and ends in authoritarianism, even if it starts with the best intentions. Hayek directed this not only against the Soviet Union, but also against the well-meaning planners of democracies. It is worth noting that he did not reject all public assistance: he accepted that the State should guarantee a minimum subsistence to those who needed it.</p><p>His second argument is more subtle. In a 1945 article, Hayek presented the <strong>market as an information system</strong>. The knowledge that drives an economy is spread across millions of heads: the baker knows how much bread is sold on Sundays; the farmer, how the harvest is coming along. No ministry can gather all that. Prices sum it up: if a material becomes scarce, its price rises and everyone starts saving it without needing to know why. It is like a traffic map that updates itself with what each driver does.</p><p>The American Milton Friedman, in <em>Capitalism and Freedom</em> (1962), added that economic freedom is also a condition of political freedom. If the State is the only employer, criticising the Government may cost you your job; if there are many private employers, economic power remains separate from political power and that protects the dissident. Friedman proposed concrete ideas: school vouchers so that families could choose a school and a negative income tax, a cash benefit that would replace many bureaucracies.</p><p>The best version of their position is not “everyone fend for themselves”, but this: no one knows enough to direct everyone's life, and concentrated power is dangerous even if good people wield it. Hayek received the Nobel Prize in Economics in 1974 and Friedman in 1976, which shows the weight their ideas came to have.</p>\n<p><strong>The liberty of the ancients and that of the moderns.</strong> In 1819, the liberal thinker Benjamin Constant (1767–1830) delivered a lecture in Paris with a title that is already a thesis: <em>The Liberty of the Ancients Compared with that of the Moderns</em>. More than a century before Berlin, he showed that the word ‘liberty’ has not always meant the same thing.</p><ul><li>For the ancients, to be free was to <strong>participate directly</strong> in public affairs: to deliberate in the assembly on war and peace, to vote on laws, to call magistrates to account. But that citizen, sovereign in the public square, had scarcely any independence in private life: the community watched over his customs and beliefs.</li><li>For the moderns, to be free is above all to enjoy a <strong>protected private independence</strong>: not to be arbitrarily arrested, to express opinions, to choose one’s occupation, to dispose of what is one’s own, to come and go or to meet without asking permission.</li></ul><p>Constant explains the change by circumstances. Modern states are large, and commerce and work occupy people’s time, so no one can spend the whole day in the assembly. For this reason he defends the representative system: we entrust others with managing common affairs. But he warns of a danger. If everyone shuts themselves away in their private affairs and gives up participating, whoever exercises power may gradually appropriate the rights we leave unwatched. Representation does not exempt us from remaining attentive.</p><p>Think of a residents’ association in any block of flats in Gasteiz where nobody goes to the meetings because ‘they are a bore’. After a few years, the managing agent alone decides the fees, the works and who is commissioned to carry them out, and does so to suit himself. Nobody has lost their flat, but everyone has lost control over it.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: how much time do you devote to public life, in your class, in your town or in your neighbourhood? What could your private life lose if you abandon it altogether?</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>It is not from the benevolence of the butcher, the brewer, or the baker that we expect our dinner, but from their regard to their own interest. — Adam Smith, <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> (1776), book I, chap. 2</p> </blockquote>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: is it enough for each person to pursue their own interest for society as a whole to gain, or does the market need rules and limits? It is an <strong>open debate</strong> that you will continue in Year 2, where you will see how this idea becomes one of the foundations of capitalism.</p>\n<h3>Criticisms of Neoliberalism and the Response</h3><p>Neoliberalism has received criticisms that are not only economic, but also philosophical. The most original was raised by Michel Foucault in his 1978-1979 lecture course at the Collège de France, published as <em>The Birth of Biopolitics</em>. Foucault observed that neoliberalism does not merely free the market: it proposes a new way of understanding the person. Each person comes to see themselves as an <strong>entrepreneur of the self</strong>, who invests in their “human capital” (studies, languages, health, contacts) hoping for a return. The idea came from economists such as Gary Becker. Think of how you sometimes talk about yourself: “build a personal brand”, “improve your profile”, “step out of your comfort zone”. For critics, when everything is seen as an investment, failure is experienced as individual guilt, even if it depends on circumstances that no one controls.</p><p>There are two other criticisms. The first, <strong>inequality</strong>: the French economist Thomas Piketty, in <em>Capital in the Twenty-First Century</em> (2013), gathered historical data to show that, since the 1980s, wealth has once again been concentrated in the hands of a minority in many countries. The second, <strong>commodification</strong>: Michael Sandel, in <em>What Money Can't Buy</em> (2012), asks whether everything should have a price. Paying children to read books, selling one's place in a queue or buying rights to pollute may be efficient, but perhaps it changes the meaning of what is bought: a book read for money is no longer the same.</p><p>Defenders of the market respond with weighty reasons. In recent decades, trade liberalisation coincided with the exit from extreme poverty of hundreds of millions of people, especially in Asia. Moreover, they maintain that many ills attributed to neoliberalism actually come from its opposite: companies that live off political favours, bailouts with public money or monopolies protected by the State, precisely what Smith and Hayek condemned. And on inequality they reply that what matters is not how much the rich have, but whether the poor improve. Finally, they point out that “neoliberal” is often used as a vague insult, without specifying what is being criticised.</p><p>The debate remains open: some see in the market the best guarantee of freedom and prosperity; others, a logic that, without limits, ends up invading spheres that should be governed by other values.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: are there things that should not be able to be bought or sold, even if the seller does so freely? Think of a grade, an organ, a vote or a place on a hospital waiting list. What criterion would you use to draw the line? And when you are told to invest in yourself, are they helping you to be freer or are they burdening you with all the responsibility?</p>\n<p>Among those challenges, the relationship between <strong>capitalism</strong> and the <strong>ecological crisis</strong> occupies the Canadian essayist <strong><a href=\"#biografias/klein\">Naomi Klein</a></strong> (b. 1970). She argues that certain elites take advantage of crises —a catastrophe, a war, a crash— to impose, while people are stunned, measures that in calm times would be rejected: she called it <strong>«disaster capitalism»</strong> (<em>The Shock Doctrine</em>, 2007). And she holds that curbing climate change requires changing the economic model, not just recycling (<em>This Changes Everything</em>, 2014). Those who criticise her reply that the market and technology are part of the solution, not only of the problem.</p>\n<h3>Global justice: do we have duties towards those who are far away?</h3><p>Almost all the political philosophy we have seen reasons within the borders of a state: how to share out power, rights and goods among those who live in one community. But today there is a question that spills beyond those borders. What do we owe the human beings who live on the other side of the planet, whom we will never see, whose language we do not speak and whose poverty we have not caused with our own hands? This is the problem of <strong>global justice</strong>, and it tests how far our idea of equality really reaches.</p><p>The Australian philosopher <strong>Peter Singer</strong> posed it with an example that is easy to picture. Suppose that, on your way to class, you pass a shallow pond and see a small child drowning. Wading in to pull them out would ruin your clothes and make you late, but no one would doubt that you must do it: a small inconvenience does not justify letting someone die whom you could save. Well, Singer goes on, every day children die from diseases and famines that a few euros would be enough to prevent; and distance, in itself, changes nothing from the moral point of view. If we can prevent something very bad by sacrificing something far less important, we are obliged to do it. The conclusion is uncomfortable, because it turns much of our everyday consumption into money we were obliged to give to those who need it more. Some object that to demand so much is unrealistic, and that aid, badly given, can create dependence; Singer replies that, even lowering the demand, almost all of us could do far more than we do.</p><p>The German philosopher <strong>Thomas Pogge</strong> shifts the focus from charity to <strong>responsibility</strong>. The problem, he holds, is not only that the rich do not help enough, but that the <strong>global institutions</strong> —the rules of international trade, debt, medicine patents, the way access to resources is shared out— are designed so as to keep many countries in misery while favouring the powerful. To this is added, Pogge recalls, the weight of the colonial past, which shared out each people's starting point very unequally. If that is so, world poverty is not someone else's misfortune that we might relieve out of kindness, but a harm in which we somehow take part as beneficiaries of that order; and repairing a harm one profits from obliges far more than helping out of generosity.</p><p>These ideas become urgent in the face of <strong>migration</strong>. Whoever flees hunger, war or the climate asks to enter where life is still possible, and the debate tightens at once: can a state close its doors to someone who did not choose where to be born? Here two ancient traditions clash. <strong>Cosmopolitanism</strong>, which goes back to the Cynics and Stoics who called themselves <em>kosmopolitai</em> (citizens of the world), holds that humanity is a single family and that the place of birth is morally arbitrary, pure lottery; already the Cynic Diogenes, when asked where he was from, answered that he was from the whole world. Against it, those who defend <strong>priority for compatriots</strong> recall that close bonds —family, neighbourhood, town, nation— also generate special duties, and that a community that does not first care for its own will hardly be able to care for anyone. Deciding how far the circle of the ours reaches, and whether that circle can widen without breaking, is one of the liveliest political questions of the present, which no border any longer really leaves out.</p>\n<h3>Political ecology: justice with nature and with the future</h3><p>We already saw that <strong>Naomi Klein</strong> links capitalism with the climate crisis; it is worth dwelling now on her argument. Her underlying thesis is that global warming does not spring from a greedy 'human nature' in the abstract, but from a concrete economic model that needs to grow without rest and has treated the atmosphere as a free dumping ground. That is why she speaks of <strong>climate justice</strong>: those who have polluted least —impoverished countries and the youngest generations— are those who most suffer droughts, floods and forced displacement, while the great fossil-fuel companies go on handing out profits. For Klein, then, confronting the climate is not a technical matter solved by recycling at home, but an occasion to remake the economy and ask who decides and at whose expense; hence her support for ambitious transition plans, in the manner of a 'Green New Deal', that fund clean energy and decent jobs with public money while cutting emissions.</p><p>From this diagnosis springs a disputed proposal: <strong>degrowth</strong>. Its defenders hold that on a planet of finite resources neither production nor consumption can grow without end, and that rich societies should produce and spend less, share out better what there is and measure their success by well-being and health, not by the gross domestic product rising each year; they propose, for example, shorter working days, repairing instead of throwing away and guaranteeing everyone the basics. Its critics reply that without growth it is very hard to lift anyone out of poverty and create jobs, and trust that clean technology will allow a 'green' growth that separates progress from ecological harm. The tug-of-war between the two positions remains unresolved.</p><p>The ecological crisis obliges us, besides, to stretch justice through time. Do we have duties towards those not yet born? This is <strong>intergenerational justice</strong>. The German philosopher <strong>Hans Jonas</strong> held that the immense power of modern technology has transformed ethics itself: it is no longer enough not to harm my neighbour of today, because my acts now can ruin the lives of beings who will come in centuries and who can neither protest nor vote. Jonas adds that, faced with harms that could be irreversible, it is wise to give more weight to pessimistic forecasts than to optimistic ones: better to err on the side of caution when what is at stake has no way back. Hence his <strong>principle of responsibility</strong>, which reformulates Kant's imperative with an eye on the future:</p><blockquote class=\"cita\"><p>Act so that the consequences of your conduct are compatible with the permanence of genuine human life on Earth. — Hans Jonas, <em>The Imperative of Responsibility</em> (1979), own translation</p></blockquote><p>Others go even further and ask whether only human beings really count. The Norwegian philosopher <strong>Arne Naess</strong> distinguished a 'shallow' ecology, which protects nature because it is useful to us and we do not want to be left without it, from a <strong>deep ecology</strong>, which recognises in other living beings a value <em>in themselves</em>, beyond the profit they give us. From that view, reducing our pressure on the Earth would not be a sacrifice, but a richer and humbler way of inhabiting it, and from it drink much of today's environmentalism and those who demand protecting rivers, forests or animals for their own good. Those who criticise it fear, by contrast, that putting the human species on the same level as the rest of life may end up playing down very urgent human needs, such as that of millions of people still struggling to escape poverty.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: faced with a crisis that affects us all, who should decide the measures, and with what oversight, so that no one turns them to their own advantage? You will meet Klein in Year 2, among the debates of the 21st century; it is an <strong>open debate</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: the old question beats here of whether one can grow without limit on a finite planet, and that of whether we have real duties towards those who are far away or not yet born. Some answer that our obligations begin with the near and that technical progress will solve the rest; others, that equality stops neither at borders nor at our generation, and that to wait without acting is already a decision. There is no verdict: it is an <strong>open debate</strong> that runs through all the politics of the twenty-first century.</p>\n<p>The Polish sociologist and philosopher <strong><a href=\"#biografias/bauman\">Zygmunt Bauman</a></strong> (1925-2017) named a feeling of the age: <strong>«liquid modernity»</strong>. What used to be solid —a job for life, lasting bonds, stable institutions— has turned <strong>liquid</strong>: everything changes fast, nothing settles. That brings freedom, but also <strong>uncertainty</strong>, and a life often turned into pure consumption, in which even relationships are used and thrown away. Bauman did not idealise the past; he described a present for us to think about.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: is a life with fewer ties and more options better, even if it is more uncertain, or do we lose something valuable when nothing lasts? You will meet Bauman in Year 2, in the philosophy of the 21st century.</p>\n<h3>Social movements and civil disobedience</h3>\n<p>Not all politics goes through parties and the ballot box. <strong>Social movements</strong> —suffragism, civil rights, environmentalism, feminism, the labour movement— organise people outside the institutions in order to change laws and customs. Many rights that seem obvious today were won in this way, through sustained social pressure before reaching parliaments.</p>\n<p>One of its tools is <strong>civil disobedience</strong>: breaking, in a <strong>public, non-violent and deliberate</strong> way, a law regarded as unjust, while accepting the penalty, in order to appeal to the conscience of the majority. Thoreau defended it by refusing to pay a tax that sustained slavery and war; Gandhi and Martin Luther King turned it into a mass strategy. It differs from ordinary crime in that it seeks neither personal gain nor concealment, but to change a rule by appealing to shared principles. Whether the law must always be obeyed, or when it is legitimate to disobey it, is an open debate.</p>\n<h2>8. The contract theorists, one by one: what is given up and what is not</h2>\n<p>The table in section 2 summarises which State comes out of each pact. Let us now see <em>why</em> each author reasons as he does and what he reserves, because in every contract there is something that is not signed away.</p>\n<h3>Reading the authors in their own words</h3><p>The tables and summaries provide the skeleton; now it is worth listening closely to a few sentences from each thinker, because nuance often hides in the text. Let us begin with the one who opens the series. For <strong>Hobbes</strong>, the covenant does not consist merely in ‘handing over’ force: it consists in <strong>authorising</strong>. Each person, moved by fear of a violent death, renounces their right to govern themselves and appoints as their representative a human being or an assembly, on condition that the others do the same at the same time. From this simultaneous act arises something that did not previously exist: a single artificial person who speaks and decides on behalf of all.</p><blockquote class=\"cita\">‘I authorise and give up my right of governing myself to this man, or to this assembly of men, on this condition, that thou give up thy right to him, and authorise all his actions in like manner.’ (Hobbes, <em>Leviathan</em>, ch. XVII)</blockquote><p>Hobbes calls this result a ‘mortal god’, subject only to the immortal God: a power capable of inspiring enough respect to keep each person in check. There is one decisive detail: the sovereign <strong>does not sign</strong> the covenant, because the agreement is sealed among the subjects themselves. For this reason he cannot break it or commit injustice against them in the strict sense, and for this very reason —his critics will say— he finds himself placed above that which he was meant to order. Understanding this move helps us see why, for Hobbes, dividing or distributing that power already amounts to beginning to dissolve it.</p><p>At bottom lies fear, but not just any fear: the reasoned dread of insecurity, because without a State each person's fate rests in their own hands. This covenant, therefore, is not a gift but a reciprocal commitment: I renounce because you also renounce, and vice versa.</p><p>It is also worth clarifying what Hobbes means by ‘war’. It is not a permanent battle but a <strong>climate</strong>: the known disposition to fight suffices, so long as there is no guarantee to the contrary, just as we speak of bad weather because of the threat of rain even when it is not raining non-stop. That is why the rules reason discovers —above all, seeking peace— are not enough on their own: covenants without a force to back them remain mere words, and only a power capable of enforcing them makes them reliable.</p>\n<h3>Locke: an authority received on loan and with limited functions</h3><p>Hobbes stresses authorisation; <strong>Locke</strong>, by contrast, places the emphasis on a <strong>mandate subject to conditions</strong>. No citizen signs a blank cheque on their fate: they agree to submit because they trust that the authority will carry out a specific function. For Locke, this agreement can arise in two ways. It is <strong>express</strong> when someone openly joins the political community; it is <strong>tacit</strong> when a person, without having signed any document, benefits from the roads, protection or inherited goods of a country and, by making use of them, in practice accepts its norms for as long as they live there.</p><blockquote class=\"cita\">‘Thus the great and chief end, therefore, of men’s uniting into commonwealths, and putting themselves under government, is the preservation of their property’, understood as life, liberty and estate. (Locke, <em>Two Treatises of Government</em>, II, §124; our translation)</blockquote><p>For this reason the functions of government are <strong>limited</strong>: its purpose is to safeguard those goods, not to use them as it pleases. Even the legislative power, however exalted it may be, is not without limits: it is obliged to govern by fixed laws known to all, to entrust their application to neutral courts, and not to deprive anyone of what is theirs without their consent. A common misunderstanding must be cleared up here: Locke <strong>indeed</strong> distinguished functions —the <strong>legislative</strong>, responsible for making laws and convened only periodically, and the <strong>executive</strong>, in permanent operation, which enforces them (in addition to a ‘federative’ power concerned with external relations, war and agreements)—. What does not belong to him is the tripartite scheme with an autonomous judiciary as its third branch: that design would be established by Montesquieu some fifty years later. And precisely because these functions are limited, if rulers fail to carry them out, the people may revoke the trust placed in them and oppose them.</p><p>Locke does not start from absolute disorder, but from a natural situation governed by a <strong>law of nature</strong>, accessible to reason, which forbids attacking the existence, liberty or possessions of others. The difficulty lies not in the absence of norms, but in the fact that each person acts as <strong>judge in their own case</strong>: since there is no shared mediator, offences are settled with favouritism and a desire for revenge. Through the pact, people agree from then on to take decisions by <strong>majority</strong> and to submit to neutral judges; for this reason government resembles less a master than a manager in whom trust is placed.</p>\n<p><strong>Hobbes</strong> (<em>Leviathan</em>, 1651) starts from the premise that human beings are <strong>roughly equal</strong>: nobody is so strong that they cannot be killed by the weakest, through cunning or by allying with others. That equality, and not an innate wickedness, makes life without a State dangerous. Hence three causes of conflict: <strong>competition</strong>, <strong>diffidence</strong> and desire for <strong>glory</strong>. Without a common power, life would be ‘solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short’ (Hobbes, <em>Leviathan</em>, ch. XIII). Moreover, the sovereign may be a king or an assembly, although Hobbes prefers monarchy; and nobody gives up the right to <strong>preserve their own life</strong>, so that the subject may resist whoever comes to kill them and owes no further obedience to a sovereign who can no longer protect them.</p>\n<p>Behind Hobbes's pact lies an idea about human motivation. <strong>Psychological egoism</strong> states, as a fact, that we all seek our own interest above all; <strong>ethical egoism</strong> goes a step further and holds that we <strong>ought</strong> to do so. Hobbes relies above all on the first: if each pursues their self-preservation and their advantages, the war of all against all is the predictable result, and precisely out of well-understood self-interest it suits us all to give up part of our freedom and make a pact for a common power. It is a powerful argument, but it has a classic weak spot: from the fact that we <strong>do seek</strong> our interest it does not follow that we <strong>ought</strong> to seek it, nor that we are not also capable of cooperating and caring for others.</p>\n<p><strong>Locke</strong> (<em>Two Treatises of Government</em>, 1690) is the philosopher of <strong>property</strong>: each person owns their own person and their labour, and whoever mixes their labour with something that belonged to nobody (cultivating a field, gathering fruit) makes it theirs. He set two limits: not to accumulate more than one can use before it spoils, and to leave enough, and as good, for others. <strong>Money</strong>, which does not rot, made it possible to accumulate without that brake, and Locke accepted the resulting inequalities. Government is a power <strong>entrusted</strong> by citizens to protect their rights and revocable if it betrays them. Note: the classic division into three powers, with an independent judiciary, is not his, but <strong>Montesquieu’s</strong> (1748).</p>\n<p>Locke's theory of property has two <strong>limits</strong> that are often forgotten: not to accumulate so much that what is not used spoils, and to leave <strong>enough and as good</strong> for others. Locke thought that the invention of <strong>money</strong>, by allowing value to be kept without spoiling and accepted by the tacit agreement of all, in fact lifted those limits and opened the door to inequality. There is, moreover, an uncomfortable shadow: the very philosopher of rights invested in the slave trade and took part in a colonial constitution that upheld it, something his own theory cannot justify. Years later, Jefferson —who drafted a Declaration inspired by Locke while himself owning slaves— would acknowledge that debt of ideas in a letter of 1825. The lesson holds for the whole subject: a theory is judged by its arguments, not by the biography of whoever signs it, but it is best not to hide the contradictions.</p>\n<p><strong>Rousseau</strong> (<em>Discourse on the Origin of Inequality</em>, 1755) maintains that natural human beings are not wicked and that inequality is born of <strong>amour-propre</strong>, the need to compare ourselves and to be better regarded than others (the expression ‘noble savage’, however, is not his). In <em>The Social Contract</em> (1762) he separates the <strong>general will</strong>, which seeks the common interest, from the <strong>will of all</strong>, a mere sum of particular interests (II, 3). The sovereignty of the people is <strong>inalienable</strong>: it is neither handed over nor represented, and the government is a mere agent whom the people can dismiss. The disturbing point is that whoever refuses to obey the general will ‘will be forced to be free’ (I, 7): hence the suspicion, already seen with Berlin, that positive liberty can turn into coercion.</p>\n<h3>Rousseau: giving oneself to everyone so as not to depend on anyone</h3><p>The previous section already showed the difference between the general will and the will of all. <strong>Rousseau</strong>’s foundational text adds one more piece: the exact form of the pact. The problem it poses is almost a political riddle.</p><blockquote class=\"cita\">‘To find a form of association which will defend and protect with the whole common force the person and goods of each associate, and in which each one, while uniting himself with all, may still obey himself alone, and remain as free as before.’ (Rousseau, <em>The Social Contract</em>, I, 6)</blockquote><p>His solution is a <strong>total surrender</strong>: each person gives themselves entirely, with all their rights, to the community as a whole. It seems like an enormous loss, but Rousseau argues the opposite. Since everyone surrenders equally and to no one in particular, no one falls under the domination of another person; one depends only on the common body of which one is oneself a part. And since that body expresses itself through the <strong>law</strong> —the general will put in writing—, obeying the law is, at bottom, obeying oneself. This is how his most cited thesis is understood: freedom does not disappear on entering society, but changes its nature, moving from the whim of the isolated individual to the autonomy of someone who gives themselves their own rules. The unsettling reverse, already noted, is what happens to whoever disagrees with that common will. This is why Rousseau insists that sovereignty is indivisible: making the law cannot be left in the hands of any representative.</p><p>From this idea arises a double requirement concerning the law: it must be <strong>general</strong> both in its origin and in its scope, that is, it must arise from the whole people and apply to everyone without exception, with no names or surnames attached. This is why Rousseau carefully separates two figures we tend to confuse: the <strong>sovereign people</strong>, the sole legislator, which cannot delegate that task, and the <strong>government</strong>, a mere executor charged with applying the laws and always revocable. He even admits an extraordinary <strong>lawgiver</strong> who helps draft good laws at the outset, but clarifies that this figure does not command: they only propose, and it is the people who approve.</p><p>Even so, Rousseau acknowledges that the general will can <strong>err</strong> in calculating where the common good lies, although its intention is always upright; this is why calm deliberation and good information matter as much as the counting of votes. It is not a matter of adding up whims, but of seeking together what truly suits everyone, and that demands attentive citizens who are not easily manipulated.</p>\n<h3>A fourth voice: Spinoza and democracy</h3><p>Textbooks usually close contractualism with the trio above, but <strong>Baruch Spinoza</strong> (1632-1677) deserves a place, for he reasons with different tools. For him, power rests less on a pact that cancels the individual than on the <strong>joint power</strong> of the multitude: a State is worth what the forces that truly sustain it are worth. From there he reaches a conclusion striking for his time. In his <em>Political Treatise</em> he compares forms of government and argues that <strong>democracy</strong> is the most natural regime, the one that least departs from the freedom nature grants each person, because in it no one transfers their right to the point of ceasing to count.</p><blockquote class=\"cita\">‘The end of the State is not to dominate men, nor to restrain them by fear (...); its end is, in truth, liberty.’ (Spinoza, <em>Theological-Political Treatise</em>, ch. XX)</blockquote><p>For Spinoza, that liberty includes the freedom to <strong>think and to express what one thinks</strong>. A government that seeks to control consciences is not only unjust but also clumsy: it provokes resistance and makes enemies needlessly. The peace he cares about is not the silence of a frightened population, but the concord of people who may disagree out loud. Against Hobbes’s Leviathan, which buys security at the price of silence, Spinoza suggests that a State is stronger, not weaker, when it lets people speak.</p><p>The coherence between his life and his philosophy is striking. Spinoza earned his bread <strong>grinding lenses</strong>, was harshly expelled from his community for his ideas, and refused a professorship so as not to compromise his independence. He observed that powers manage people through two springs, <strong>fear</strong> and <strong>hope</strong>, and that a State sustained only by fear ends up fragile. His defence of tolerance and free inquiry nourished, decades later, much of Enlightenment thought.</p><p>There is in Spinoza a radical idea about <strong>right</strong>: each thing has as much right as it has power, so that no one ever fully renounces their capacity to judge for themselves. A regime may forbid people to say certain things, but it will not manage to stop them thinking them; whoever tries only breeds hypocrites and resentment. Hence, for him, freedom of conscience is not a luxury but a condition of stability.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Author</th><th>What is their fear?</th><th>What cannot be renounced?</th><th>When is it legitimate to disobey?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Hobbes</strong></td><td>Disorder and dying violently</td><td>The right of each person to preserve their life</td><td>Only if the sovereign endangers your life or is unable to continue protecting you</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Locke</strong></td><td>That each person judges matters that affect them</td><td>Property, liberty and life</td><td>If the government violates the rights whose protection it was entrusted with (<strong>right of resistance</strong>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rousseau</strong></td><td>Inequality and dependence on others</td><td>One’s own sovereignty, which cannot be delegated</td><td>If the government appropriates the sovereignty that belongs to the people</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Montesquieu: checking power with power</h3><p>We have already compared the three contractualists in the table; now <strong>Montesquieu</strong> (1689-1755) adds what remained to be resolved. Locke distinguished between the power that makes the rules and the one that executes them; Montesquieu introduces a third, the <strong>judicial</strong>, and also makes a decisive turn: dividing tasks is not enough, each power must <strong>check</strong> the others. He had observed that whoever concentrates authority tends, almost without realising it, to expand it further and further.</p><blockquote class=\"cita\">‘The experience of all ages teaches that any man with power tends to abuse it (...). To prevent the abuse of power, it is necessary that, by the very arrangement of things, power should check power.’ (Montesquieu, <em>The Spirit of the Laws</em>, XI, 4)</blockquote><p>This is called <strong>checks and balances</strong>: every power has the means to contain the abuses of the others, and thus liberty no longer depends on the good intentions of the ruler, but rests on the balance between the parts. Almost all the constitutions that came afterwards adopted this principle, which, together with Spinoza's faith in citizens who express themselves freely, shapes what we will call the liberal tradition.</p><p>There is also a second contribution from Montesquieu that calls contractualism into question: according to him, no model suits all places equally, since laws depend on the particular <strong>‘spirit’</strong> of each people, that is, on its climate, its customs, its beliefs, its commercial activity and its past. He also orders the types of government according to the principle that drives them: the <strong>republic</strong> rests on the virtue of the citizens, the <strong>monarchy</strong> on honour and <strong>despotism</strong> on fear. Contrasting this approach with Rousseau's general will or with Locke's contract is, in itself, a worthwhile exercise.</p><p><strong>For debate (no closed answer):</strong> these theories leave us as a legacy, at least, two questions that are still open. The first comes from Hobbes and Locke: <em>what part of our liberty would we accept surrendering in order to feel safe?</em> After an attack or a pandemic, many people accept surveillance that they would reject under normal circumstances; for some it is simple prudence, for others, the beginning of a possible abuse. The second arises from Rousseau: <em>is it really possible to know what the general will is, or does it end up coinciding with what is claimed by whoever presents themselves as its spokesperson?</em> Some believe that deliberation and the ballot box reveal it; others suspect that it may conceal the interests of a minority. It is not a matter of passing judgement: what is valuable is that you support your opinion with reasons and attend to those of people who think differently from you.</p>\n<p>Today, someone who after a war accepts almost any authority in order to recover security reasons like Hobbes; someone who protests because a government cuts freedoms without justification appeals to Locke; someone who demands a referendum because ‘the people must decide that’ speaks like Rousseau. <strong>Key idea: all three found power on consent, but they disagree about what is given up and what always remains in the hands of each person or of the people.</strong></p>\n<h3>The criticisms of the contractualists</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Against Hobbes:</strong> he paints human beings too darkly; his solution, an almost unlimited power, opens the door to abuse and authoritarianism; and it is odd that the sovereign, who does not sign the pact, should stand above it.</li><li><strong>Against Locke:</strong> he grounds rights in our being God's work, which leaves them without a basis for those who do not believe; no one agrees on what exactly natural law dictates; and his defence of property reflects the point of view of owners.</li><li><strong>Against Rousseau:</strong> how do we know what the general will is and not the mere sum of interests?; and his idea that someone can be ‘forced to be free’ can serve as an alibi for crushing the individual in the name of the whole.</li></ul>\n<h2>9. Redistribute or respect what has been acquired? Rawls versus Nozick</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-60\">Section 4 presented the <strong>veil of ignorance</strong> of <strong>John Rawls</strong> (<em>A Theory of Justice</em>, 1971). In that <strong>original position</strong>, those who choose the rules do not know what place they will occupy, but they do know that everyone needs certain <strong>primary goods</strong>: liberties, opportunities, income and the social bases of self-respect. The rational choice would be the <strong>maximin</strong> rule: choosing the option whose worst outcome is the least bad, because nobody would stake their whole life on a roulette wheel in which they might end up in misery.</p>\n<p>His principles have a strict order: first, <strong>equal basic liberties</strong>, which are not exchanged for wealth; then, economic inequalities are acceptable only if they come with <strong>fair equality of opportunity</strong> and benefit the least advantaged (the <strong>difference principle</strong>). Nobody deserves to have been born with more talent or into a richer family: that lottery is <strong>morally arbitrary</strong>. That a doctor earns more can be just if that incentive improves healthcare for those who are worst off.</p>\n<p><strong>Robert Nozick</strong>, Rawls’s colleague at Harvard, replied in <em>Anarchy, State, and Utopia</em> (1974) with an <strong>entitlement theory</strong>: a distribution is just if each person acquired what is theirs legitimately or received it through free exchanges and gifts. What matters is not the shape of the final distribution but its <strong>history</strong>. His example: starting from a perfect distribution, thousands of fans freely pay a little extra to see a great basketball player (Wilt Chamberlain), who ends up much richer. Where is the injustice? That is why Nozick defends a <strong>minimal State</strong> and even equated taxes on earnings from labour with forced labour.</p>\n<p>A third voice, <strong>communitarianism</strong> (Michael Sandel, Alasdair MacIntyre, Michael Walzer, although several rejected the label), criticises both for starting from an abstract individual. Sandel objects that whoever chooses behind the veil is an ‘unencumbered self’ who does not exist: we are sons and daughters, neighbours and members of a tradition, and what is just cannot be thought of apart from the goods we share.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Rawls</strong></th><th><strong>Nozick</strong></th><th><strong>Communitarianism</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What makes a society just?</td><td>Rules we would accept without knowing who we are going to be</td><td>That acquisitions and free exchanges are respected</td><td>That it responds to the goods and values the community shares</td></tr><tr><td>Role of the State</td><td>Correcting inequalities (education, healthcare, taxes)</td><td>Minimal: protecting rights and contracts</td><td>Looking after life in common and participation</td></tr><tr><td>Objection it receives</td><td>Imposes sacrifices on those who earned what is theirs honestly</td><td>Ignores that nobody starts from the same square</td><td>Can stifle those who do not share the group’s values</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>The criticisms of Rawls</h3>\n<p>Rawls's proposal has met objections worth knowing. <strong>First:</strong> the veil of ignorance would be <strong>unworkable</strong>, since no one really thinks while forgetting who they are, so the ‘impartial’ choice is only a thought experiment. <strong>Second:</strong> it is not as neutral as it seems, for it takes for granted <strong>liberal values</strong> —it puts liberty first— which not all traditions share. <strong>Third</strong>, that of the utilitarians: if what is at stake is well-being, the right thing would sometimes be to seek the greatest good of the whole, even if it does not benefit the worst-off first. <strong>Fourth:</strong> securing the most disadvantaged group (the <em>maximin</em> rule) is not the only rational conduct behind the veil; one might take a risk hoping for a greater gain. It is worth recalling, though, that Rawls's first principle already requires the <strong>rule of law</strong> and equal basic liberties for all.</p>\n<p>The same happens with every inheritance tax: does it level the starting point or invade what was legitimately earned? <strong>Key idea: Rawls looks at the outcome (how do those who are worst off fare?), Nozick at history (how was each thing acquired?) and communitarianism at context (which goods do we share?).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Try it:</strong> the <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-velo\">veil of ignorance dilemma</a> asks you to choose the rules of a society without knowing what place you will occupy in it; it is precisely the thought experiment with which Rawls defends his principles of justice.</p>\n<h2>10. Models of democracy and their threats</h2>\n<p>There are several models of democracy depending on what is expected of citizens. In <strong>representative liberal democracy</strong>, the people periodically elect those who decide and the Constitution protects rights that no majority can touch; for Joseph <strong>Schumpeter</strong> (1942), its great virtue is that rulers can be changed without violence. <strong>Deliberative democracy</strong>, with <strong>Jürgen Habermas</strong> (<em>Between Facts and Norms</em>, 1992) as its great theorist, holds that a decision is legitimate if it has first been discussed in public, under conditions of freedom and equality, so that the better argument counts and not money. <strong>Participatory democracy</strong> (Carole Pateman, with roots in Rousseau) asks that citizens decide directly what affects them.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>The essentials</th><th>Role of the citizen</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Representative liberal</strong></td><td>Free elections, rights and control of power</td><td>Voting and demanding accountability</td><td>General elections every four years</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Deliberative</strong></td><td>Reasoned public debate before deciding</td><td>Arguing and listening</td><td>The citizens’ assembly in Ireland (2016-2018), which deliberated on abortion before the 2018 referendum</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Participatory</strong></td><td>Direct decision on local matters</td><td>Getting involved and deciding</td><td>Participatory budgeting (Porto Alegre, Brazil, since 1989)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every democracy has threats. The <strong>tyranny of the majority</strong>, an expression spread by <strong>Tocqueville</strong> (<em>Democracy in America</em>, 1835-1840) and <strong>John Stuart Mill</strong> (<em>On Liberty</em>, 1859), is the oppression of a minority with all the legal formalities or through social pressure; against it stand fundamental rights and constitutional courts. <strong>Populism</strong>, according to Cas Mudde’s influential definition, divides society into a ‘pure people’ and a ‘corrupt elite’; its decisive feature, Jan-Werner Müller stresses, is <strong>anti-pluralism</strong>: whoever disagrees is no longer a legitimate opponent but an enemy of the people. And there is <strong>erosion from within</strong>: rather than through coups, democracies today decay when elected governments gradually weaken judges, the press and electoral referees (Steven Levitsky and Daniel Ziblatt, <em>How Democracies Die</em>, 2018).</p>\n<h3>Deliberating and participating: two ways of deepening democracy</h3><p>You already know the models. Let us now see how they work and what they are criticised for. The central idea of <strong>deliberative democracy</strong> is that a decision is not legitimate just because it won a vote count, but because of the path that led to it. Habermas describes ideal conditions: that all those affected can speak, that no one is excluded, that there are no threats or bribes, and that only reasons others can accept count. Under those conditions, the only legitimate pressure is that of the better argument. The difference from simply voting is great. Voting <em>adds up</em> preferences as they come; deliberating can <em>change them</em>. Think of a class deciding where to go on a school trip. If they vote immediately, what the majority already wanted wins. If they discuss first, someone may point out that a classmate in a wheelchair could not climb the mountain, and the majority changes its mind without anyone forcing it.</p><p>Its advantages: better-informed decisions, more respect for minorities, who have at least been heard, and greater acceptance of the outcome. Its problems: it is slow, and not everything can be discussed in depth. Moreover, equality in debate is more ideal than real, because those who speak better or have more education carry more weight. The philosopher Chantal Mouffe adds a fundamental criticism: politics is also conflict between interests that do not dissolve through talking, and seeking consensus can mask disagreement instead of acknowledging it.</p><p><strong>Participatory democracy</strong> stresses that people decide for themselves. Today many town councils set aside part of the budget for residents to propose and vote on projects: a park, a cycle lane, the renovation of a pelota court. Another tool revives the Athenian sortition: <strong>citizens’ assemblies</strong>. In France, the Citizens’ Convention on Climate (2019–2020) brought together 150 people chosen by lot to propose measures against climate change. In Belgium’s German-speaking Community, a permanent citizens’ council, also chosen by lot, has been operating since 2019.</p><p>Its advocates stress that it brings decisions closer to those who experience them, that it educates in responsibility, and that sortition better reflects social diversity than elections, because it does not depend on campaign money. Its critics reply that few take part in open processes, and almost always the same people: those with time and networks. They also ask to whom someone no one has elected is accountable. And they point out that if governments later ignore the proposals, participation becomes window dressing. In the French case, for example, many proposals were watered down or left out of the final law.</p>\n<h3>Criticisms of democracy and their possible responses</h3><p>Defending democracy seriously requires knowing the strongest objections, not just the caricatures. Here are six, each with a possible answer. None of these answers closes the discussion.</p><ul><li><strong>Tyranny of the majority.</strong> Besides the danger that the majority may oppress through laws, Tocqueville saw a more subtle one: the pressure of dominant opinion, which imprisons no one but isolates anyone who thinks differently, until they stop saying it. Mill called it social tyranny and considered it harder to combat than that of governments. <em>Answer:</em> in addition to rights and courts, a culture of free discussion is needed in which disagreeing does not come at a high cost.</li><li><strong>Populism.</strong> It is accused of dividing society into a good people and a bad elite, and of treating one’s rival as an enemy. <em>Answer:</em> authors such as Ernesto Laclau (<em>On Populist Reason</em>, 2005) argue that it can also give a voice to those whom institutions have left out. For them, the problem is not populism as such, but its version that does not tolerate pluralism.</li><li><strong>Apathy and abstention.</strong> The economist Anthony Downs (<em>An Economic Theory of Democracy</em>, 1957) pointed out a paradox: the probability that a single vote will decide an election is minimal, so informing oneself thoroughly hardly “pays off”. <em>Answer:</em> voting is not just a calculation, but a civic duty or a way of expressing oneself. Some countries, such as Australia, have made voting compulsory, although that too is debated.</li><li><strong>Technocracy.</strong> Faced with complex problems, such as a pandemic, inflation or energy, should experts not decide? <em>Answer:</em> the expert can say which means serve a given end, but not which end to prefer. Choosing between more security or more freedom, or between growing and protecting a valley, is a question of values in which no one has more votes for knowing more.</li><li><strong>Interest groups.</strong> Companies, associations or trade unions with more resources influence laws more than the individual citizen. <em>Answer:</em> public registers of lobbyists, limits on party funding and transparency. There are even those who, like the political scientist Robert Dahl, saw competition among many groups as a normal part of democratic life, provided that none dominates the others.</li><li><strong>Post-truth.</strong> If emotions weigh more than facts, the vote is manipulated with hoaxes. <em>Answer:</em> critical education, a free press and fact-checking. Others recall that propaganda is as old as politics, and that the solution cannot be to take the vote away from those who have been deceived.</li></ul>\n<p>A nearby symptom: branding anyone who disagrees a ‘traitor’ is anti-pluralism, wherever it comes from. <strong>Key idea: democracy is not reduced to majority rule; it needs deliberation, participation and limits that protect minorities and the institutions that control power.</strong></p>\n<h3>More democracy or better democracy?</h3><p>Faced with these criticisms, two paths of reform are open, and they do not always go together. Some call for <strong>more democracy</strong>: more referendums, more sortition, more participatory budgets, so that people decide more often and on more things. Others call for <strong>better democracy</strong>: higher-quality decisions, even if this means leaving some of them in the hands of independent institutions or better-prepared people. The most radical version of this second path has a name: <em>epistocracy</em>, the rule of those who know.</p><p>The idea is not new. Plato already wanted philosophers to govern, and John Stuart Mill, in <em>Considerations on Representative Government</em> (1861), proposed giving extra votes to the more educated, without taking the vote away from anyone. Today it is defended by the American philosopher Jason Brennan (<em>Against Democracy</em>, 2016). His argument: just as we would not accept a jury that convicts without looking at the evidence, we have a right not to be governed by incompetent decisions. Brennan distinguishes three types of voters: the “Hobbits”, who do not inform themselves; the “Hooligans”, who live politics like supporters of a team; and the “Vulcans”, who reason coldly, and who would be very few. He therefore proposes, for example, political knowledge tests in order to vote.</p><p>Defenders of <strong>political equality</strong> respond in several ways. David Estlund (<em>Democratic Authority</em>, 2008) admits that some know more than others, but asks who decides who the wise are: any criterion would be contestable among reasonable people, and no one has any reason to accept that their vote should count for less. History, moreover, fuels suspicion: in the southern United States, literacy tests were used to prevent black people from voting. Hélène Landemore (<em>Democratic Reason</em>, 2013) goes further: a large and varied group can get things right more often than a small group of experts, because it brings together perspectives that experts do not have. Condorcet (1785) had already shown that, if each voter has more than a fifty per cent chance of being right, the majority is right almost always when the group is large. The problem is that the same theorem works in reverse: if each person is wrong more often than right, the majority will almost certainly be wrong.</p><p>Between these two extremes there are mixed proposals. James Fishkin’s deliberative polls inform a randomly selected sample and have them debate before asking their opinion. The Belgian writer David Van Reybrouck (<em>Against Elections</em>, 2013) proposes combining elected parliaments with chambers chosen by lot. Some see in them the best of both sides; others, a mere patch.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: if your school had to decide the exam timetable, would it be voted on by all the students, by a randomly selected commission that first listened to all parties, or by the teachers, who know the syllabus better? Would your answer change if the decision were about a country’s energy policy? What would have to happen for you to accept that your vote counted less than someone else’s, or for you to accept that it counted more?</p>\n<p><strong>The representative’s craft.</strong> Whoever governs or sits in a parliament needs <strong>political legitimacy</strong>: to have been elected cleanly, to act according to procedures and to give an account of what they do. They do not, by contrast, need <strong>moral authority</strong> to decree which life is good and which is bad. In a plural society believers and non-believers live together, as do people with very different ideas about the family, work or happiness. The representative’s task is not to impose one of those visions, but to arbitrate between diverse interests and conceptions so that all can live together under common rules.</p><p>That is why their work is made up of negotiation, pacts and concessions. To give way is not to betray: it is part of the craft when no group has all the power. Max Weber, in <em>Politics as a Vocation</em> (1919), distinguished between the <em>ethics of conviction</em>, which acts according to principles without looking too much at the consequences, and the <em>ethics of responsibility</em>, which requires one to answer for the foreseeable effects of what one does. For Weber, the good politician does not renounce their convictions, but neither can they ignore the results: both ethics must go together.</p><p>It is worth separating two things that resemble each other. A <strong>legitimate compromise</strong> is made openly, on matters of general interest, and can be explained to voters. An <strong>opaque deal</strong> is made in secret and exchanges support for personal or party favours that nobody would dare defend in public.</p><p>An example: a mayor without a majority needs to pass the budget. They make a pact with two other groups; they give up a project they had promised, and the others agree to increase the school transport budget line. Afterwards they publish the full text of the agreement on the municipal website. They have given way, but anyone can judge what they gave and what they obtained.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is a politician who gives way on part of their programme inconsistent? And what about one who never gives way on anything, even if that blocks the institutions?</p>\n<p>To these threats, the French philosopher <strong><a href=\"#biografias/deleuze\">Gilles Deleuze</a></strong> (1925-1995) added a new one. Where Foucault had described <strong>disciplinary societies</strong> (the school, the factory, the prison, which confine and watch), Deleuze announced the <strong>«societies of control»</strong> (1990): there is no longer any need to confine, because control is <strong>continuous and at a distance</strong> —passwords, profiles, digital traces— and follows us everywhere without walls. It is not science fiction: every time an app records where you are and what you buy, that invisible control is at work.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: if in exchange for security and comfort we accept being watched all the time, is such a life still free? Where would you draw the line? You will meet Deleuze in Year 2, in postmodernity.</p>\n<h3>Active citizenship</h3>\n<p>Voting every so often is the <strong>minimum</strong> of democratic life, not its ceiling. A healthy democracy also needs an awake <strong>civil society</strong>: associations, a free press, trade unions, volunteering, neighbours who organise themselves. Citizenship is not only a list of <strong>rights</strong> to claim, but also of <strong>duties</strong> —keeping informed, taking part, respecting common rules, paying fair taxes—, and much of that participation happens between one election and the next. Where people stop caring about the common good, the space they leave empty is taken up by others.</p>\n<h2>11. Utopias and dystopias: imagining societies to judge our own</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-74\">The word <strong>utopia</strong> was coined by Thomas <strong>More</strong> in 1516 with a Greek pun: <em>ou-topos</em>, ‘no place’, sounds almost the same as <em>eu-topos</em>, ‘good place’. A utopia describes a non-existent ideal society; a <strong>dystopia</strong>, an undesirable one that seems the logical consequence of present trends.</p>\n<p>The first great philosophical utopia is the just city of the <em>Republic</em> of <strong>Plato</strong>, governed by <strong>philosopher-kings</strong>, because only those who know the Good know how to govern; its guardians would live without private property or families of their own, so that nothing would distract them from the common good. It is a model for finding one’s bearings, whether or not it exists. In More’s <em>Utopia</em> there is no private property and people work six hours a day, but there are slaves, a reminder that every utopia bears the marks of its age.</p>\n<h3>Inside the Renaissance Utopias</h3><p><em>Utopia</em> by <strong>Thomas More</strong> (1516) has two books, and the first is often forgotten. It is a conversation about the England of his time in which the traveller Raphael Hythloday denounces the <strong>enclosures</strong>: large landowners fenced off common lands to raise sheep, because wool brought in more money, and drove out the peasants. In a famous image, he says that sheep, formerly gentle, have become so ravenous that they devour men. Afterwards those landless peasants steal in order to eat, and the law hangs them. Hythloday asks what kind of justice it is that first manufactures thieves and then punishes them. Only then, in Book II, does he describe the island: common property, a six-hour working day, houses exchanged by lot, gold so despised that it is used for chamber pots and chains, and <strong>religious tolerance</strong>, in a Europe on the point of being torn apart by the wars of religion.</p><p><strong><a href=\"#biografias/campanella\">Tommaso Campanella</a></strong> wrote <em>The City of the Sun</em> in 1602, imprisoned in Naples after a conspiracy against Spanish rule in Calabria. His city is governed by a priest-philosopher, and everything is held in common, including the education of children; the walls are painted with knowledge so that people learn as they walk. He criticises a society in which a few accumulate and many live in misery or idleness.</p><p><em>New Atlantis</em> by <strong>Francis Bacon</strong>, published in 1627, after his death, barely speaks of laws: its centre is the <strong>Solomon’s House</strong>, an institution of learned men who investigate nature through experiments in order to improve human life. Bacon criticises a kind of learning based on books and disputes that was of no practical use. Curiously, those learned men decide on their own which inventions they reveal to the State and which they do not.</p>\n<p>The 20th century, after the world wars and the totalitarianisms, preferred dystopias. In <em>Brave New World</em> (1932), Aldous <strong>Huxley</strong> imagines a stable society where human beings are manufactured in laboratories, divided into castes, conditioned from the cradle and calmed with a drug, <em>soma</em>. In <em>1984</em> (1949), George <strong>Orwell</strong> describes a Party that watches every gesture through the telescreen, rewrites the past and impoverishes the language with <strong>Newspeak</strong> so that certain ideas become unthinkable. Neil Postman (<em>Amusing Ourselves to Death</em>, 1985) condensed the contrast: Orwell feared that books would be banned; Huxley, that nobody would want to read them any more.</p>\n<h3>Two More Dystopias: Zamyatin and Atwood</h3><p>Before Huxley and Orwell there was a pioneering dystopia. The Russian <strong>Yevgeny Zamyatin</strong> wrote <em>We</em> in 1920-1921, a few years after the 1917 Revolution, and the Soviet censorship would not allow it to be published in his country: it first appeared translated into English in 1924. In the One State people have no names, only numbers, such as D-503, the protagonist; they live in glass houses, so that everything can be seen, and follow a common timetable that sets the hours for eating, working and walking. The mechanism of control is a <strong>mathematical rationality</strong> that promises happiness in exchange for freedom: freedom, it is said, would be the source of disorder and suffering. In the end, the State offers an operation that eradicates the imagination. That is why it is still read: it raises the question of whether a life calculated in detail, transparent and efficient, can remain human.</p><p>The Canadian <strong>Margaret Atwood</strong> published <em>The Handmaid's Tale</em> in 1985. After a coup d'état in the United States, the Republic of Gilead imposes a theocracy; since the birth rate has fallen, fertile women are assigned as ‘handmaids’ to powerful men to bear them children. They lose their names (the narrator is called ‘of Fred’), cannot read and wear red. Here control is exercised over the <strong>body</strong> and justified by a sacred text. Atwood has explained that she included nothing that had not already happened somewhere in history. The 2017 television series brought it back to the centre, and the red dress appears in protests of very different kinds: some read it as a warning about women's rights; others, about the mixing of religion and political power, or about any regime that decides for us what a good life is.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Work</th><th>How are people controlled?</th><th>What is lost?</th><th>Present-day echo</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Huxley</strong>, <em>Brave New World</em> (1932)</td><td>With <strong>pleasure</strong>, consumption and conditioning</td><td>Freedom and critical thinking, without anyone missing them</td><td>Endless entertainment, screens designed so that you never put them down</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Orwell</strong>, <em>1984</em> (1949)</td><td>With <strong>fear</strong>, surveillance and propaganda</td><td>Truth, privacy and memory</td><td>Mass surveillance, manipulation of language and of facts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>What are they for? Utopias fulfil a <strong>critical</strong> function (they expose the defects of real society) and an <strong>orienting</strong> one (they point to a horizon); dystopias are <strong>warnings</strong> that exaggerate a present trend so that we see it in time. Karl <strong>Popper</strong> (<em>The Open Society and Its Enemies</em>, 1945) warned that imposing a perfect society ends up justifying any means against whoever does not fit, and preferred gradual reforms against concrete evils. <strong>Key idea: utopias and dystopias do not predict the future; they are thought experiments for judging the present: utopia shows what could improve and dystopia, what we could lose.</strong></p>\n<h3>Dangerous or Necessary? Popper versus Bloch</h3><p>The Austrian <strong>Karl Popper</strong> wrote <em>The Open Society and Its Enemies</em> (1945) during the Second World War, and in it he distinguishes two ways of reforming society. <strong>Utopian social engineering</strong> starts from a complete blueprint of the ideal society and tries to build it all at once. <strong>Piecemeal engineering</strong> seeks concrete evils —unemployment, illness, poverty— and attacks them with small reforms that can be evaluated and corrected. His argument against the first is strong: a total plan requires power concentrated for a long time; if the plan fails, no one can know in time, because criticism is experienced as sabotage; and since the end is perfect, any means seems justified against anyone who does not fit in. No one knows with certainty what the perfect society is like, but we all recognise concrete suffering.</p><p>The German <strong>Ernst Bloch</strong> responded from a different standpoint in <em>The Principle of Hope</em> (three volumes, 1954-1959). For him, the human being is not finished: it lives by reaching towards what <strong>is not yet</strong>. That anticipation appears in daydreams, fairy tales, music, medicine and architecture. Bloch distinguishes abstract utopia, pure desire without roots, from <strong>concrete utopia</strong>, which discovers real possibilities in the present. His best reason: without utopia, we would accept what exists as the only possibility, and many achievements —the end of slavery, universal suffrage— were utopias before they became laws.</p><p>Popper's supporters reply that boundless hope has served as an alibi for the worst regimes; Bloch's, that prudence without a horizon ends in conformism.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can a society be improved without first imagining how it ought to be? And can one imagine a perfect society without the temptation to impose it?</p>\n<h2>12. Feminism and the gender perspective</h2>\n<p><strong>Feminism</strong> is both a theory and a political movement that denounces inequality between women and men and seeks to eliminate it. Its history is usually told in <strong>waves</strong>, a useful but simplifying metaphor: it was coined in the United States in 1968 and does not fit all countries equally.</p>\n<h3>The waves told through their own texts</h3><p>The table of the waves shows dates and names, although it is also worth listening to the arguments of those who led them. The Assembly of France approved in 1789 the <em>Déclaration des droits de l'homme et du citoyen</em>. Barely two years later, <strong>Olympe de Gouges</strong>, a writer, brought to light her own reply, the <em>Déclaration des droits de la femme et de la citoyenne</em> (1791), in which she reworked the official text point by point. Its first article begins like this:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>‘Woman is born free and remains equal to man in rights’. (Olympe de Gouges, <em>Déclaration des droits de la femme…</em>, 1791, art. 1; own translation)</p></blockquote><p>Her sharpest formulation appears in the tenth article:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>‘If woman has the right to mount the scaffold, she must equally have the right to mount the rostrum’. (Olympe de Gouges, <em>Déclaration…</em>, 1791, art. 10; own translation)</p></blockquote><p>This is a reasoning based on <strong>coherence</strong>. When the law imposes a punishment on a woman, it regards her as someone responsible for her actions; it makes no sense, then, to treat her like a child when she seeks to speak before others. She must be a subject in both spheres or in neither. Events ended up proving her right in a tragic way: in 1793, at the height of the Terror, De Gouges was executed by guillotine.</p><p>The following year, from London, <strong>Mary Wollstonecraft</strong> sent to the press <em>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman</em> (1792). You already know that her work answered Rousseau; now we are concerned with the idea that underpins it. According to her, virtue has no sex, since it consists in acting in accordance with reason. And that capacity grows if it is exercised and withers if it is neglected: when a girl is trained only to please, to adorn herself and to obey, it is hardly surprising that, as an adult, she gives a superficial impression. The fault lies not in her nature, but in the education she has received. Hence she defended a national public school where boys and girls would learn in the same classrooms. About the aim of that education she wrote:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>‘I do not wish them to have power over men, but over themselves’. (Mary Wollstonecraft, <em>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman</em>, 1792, chap. IV; own translation)</p></blockquote><p>Note the difference: what Wollstonecraft demands is not turning the hierarchy upside down, but <strong>autonomy</strong>, that is, that each person should be governed by their own reason. Both thinkers resort to an identical tactic: they take seriously the universal character that the Enlightenment proclaimed and direct it against those who curtailed it. If human beings are recognised as having rights because they possess reason, what reason justifies excluding half of the species?</p>\n<h3>Second wave: from destiny to power</h3><p>With the vote and access to university achieved in many countries, an uncomfortable question remained: why, with the same rights on paper, was women's life still so different? Three books changed the way of posing it.</p><p><strong>Simone de Beauvoir</strong> (<em>The Second Sex</em>, 1949) drew on the existentialism of her milieu: the human being has no essence fixed in advance, but makes itself through its choices. Applied to women, this means that biology is not a destiny. Beauvoir distinguishes between <em>transcendence</em>, launching oneself into one's own projects that open up a future, and <em>immanence</em>, being confined to tasks that repeat themselves without leaving a work behind, like cleaning that will have to be done again tomorrow. Her accusation is that culture has reserved the former for men and assigned the latter to women, presenting it moreover as ‘natural’.</p><p>Fourteen years later, the American journalist <strong>Betty Friedan</strong> published <em>The Feminine Mystique</em> (1963). She had surveyed former university classmates and found in many suburban housewives, with house, car and appliances, a diffuse malaise that they themselves could not name. That is why she titled her first chapter “The Problem That Has No Name”. She described a woman who, at the end of the day, asked herself in silence: “Is this all?” (ch. 1). The ‘mystique’ was the advertising ideal of the happy wife who fulfils herself only in the home. Friedan co-founded in 1966 the National Organization for Women (NOW). Later she was criticised, for example by bell hooks (<em>Feminist Theory: From Margin to Center</em>, 1984), for speaking about white, middle-class women as if they were all women.</p><p><strong>Kate Millett</strong> (<em>Sexual Politics</em>, 1970) took the decisive theoretical step: she broadened the meaning of ‘politics’. Politics is not only what happens in parliament, but any relationship structured by power, in which one group dominates another. To show this, she analysed novels by widely read authors such as D. H. Lawrence, Henry Miller or Norman Mailer, and pointed out how domination was presented in them as desire or as natural order. The idea that the personal is political, which as you have seen is not her slogan, finds a theoretical basis in her book.</p><p>The trajectory is clear: Beauvoir asks how woman is made, Friedan listens to her malaise, and Millett names it as power.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Period</th><th>Main demand</th><th>Authors and texts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Antecedent in the Enlightenment</strong></td><td>Last decades of the 18th century</td><td>Extending to women the so-called rights ‘of man’; access to education</td><td>1791: Olympe de Gouges, <em>Déclaration des droits de la femme et de la citoyenne</em>; 1792: Mary Wollstonecraft, <em>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>First wave</strong></td><td>19th century and early 20th century</td><td>Suffrage, access to education and civil rights</td><td>Seneca Falls Declaration (1848); <em>The Subjection of Women</em> (1869), by John Stuart Mill; in Spain, women’s suffrage in 1931, defended by Clara Campoamor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Second wave</strong></td><td>1960s, 1970s and 1980s</td><td>Effective equality in employment, the family, sexuality and one’s own body</td><td>As an antecedent, <em>Le Deuxième Sexe</em> (1949), by Simone de Beauvoir; <em>The Feminine Mystique</em> (1963), by Betty Friedan; <em>Sexual Politics</em> (1970), by Kate Millett</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Third wave</strong></td><td>1990s</td><td>Plurality of experiences; intersectional approach</td><td>Kimberlé Crenshaw; <em>Gender Trouble</em> (1990), by Judith Butler</td></tr><tr><td><strong>A fourth wave?</strong></td><td>From 2010 onwards</td><td>Sexual assault, harassment and care work; activism through social media</td><td>#MeToo movement (2017); feminist strikes every 8 March (in Spain, from 2018 onwards)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-82\"><strong>Wollstonecraft</strong> replied to Rousseau, who in <em>Emile</em> (1762) wanted to educate women to please men: if they seem less rational, it is because they are denied education. In Spain, after women’s suffrage was approved in 1931, women voted for the first time in a general election in 1933. <strong>Beauvoir</strong> showed that culture has thought of woman as <strong>‘the Other’</strong>, always defined in relation to the male, and summed it up thus: ‘One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman’ (<em>The Second Sex</em>, 1949).</p>\n<p>Key concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Patriarchy</strong>: a social organisation in which power is exercised mainly by men. Kate Millett made it a central concept: the relationship between the sexes is a power relationship, in the family and in sexuality too. From that period comes the slogan <strong>‘the personal is political’</strong>, which became popular through an essay by Carol Hanisch with that title (written in 1969 and published in 1970); it is not Millett’s, to whom it is sometimes attributed.</li>\n<li><strong>Sex and gender</strong>: sex refers to biological traits; <strong>gender</strong>, to the roles and norms that a society assigns to each sex. The distinction spread in the 1970s (Ann Oakley, 1972). Judith Butler challenged it: for her gender is <strong>performative</strong>, produced by repeating certain norms, and is not a prior essence.</li>\n<li><strong>Equality and difference</strong>: <strong>equality feminism</strong> (in Spain, Celia Amorós or Amelia Valcárcel) sees the differences between the sexes as largely constructed and demands the same rights and opportunities; <strong>difference feminism</strong> (Luce Irigaray, for example) does not want women to resemble men, but wants what is considered feminine and despised to be valued.</li>\n<li><strong>Intersectionality</strong>: a term coined by Kimberlé Crenshaw (1989). Discriminations intersect: a poor Black woman does not suffer a simple sum of disadvantages, but a specific form of exclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Gender perspective</strong>: asking how a norm or an institution affects women and men. For example, for decades the standard crash-test dummies for cars reproduced an average male body, and the safety of women drivers was studied less well.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Third wave and beyond: intersections and norms</h3><p>In the late 1980s, the question “What do women want?” began to seem too simple: which women? Two American authors, from very different fields, complicated the answer.</p><p><strong>Kimberlé Crenshaw</strong>, a jurist, coined the term intersectionality in a 1989 article published in a law review of the University of Chicago. She started from a real case: in <em>DeGraffenreid v. General Motors</em> (1976), several black women workers sued the company for discrimination. The court dismissed the claim with a striking line of reasoning: General Motors hired women, so it did not discriminate by sex, and it hired black people, so it did not discriminate by race. The problem was that the women hired were white, in the offices, and the black people were men, in the factory. Black women did not fit into either of the two boxes, and the law did not see their situation. Crenshaw explained it with the image of a street intersection: the harm can come from one direction, from the other, or from the collision in the centre. Her lesson is methodological: if each discrimination is looked at separately, people who suffer several at once can disappear from the map.</p><p><strong>Judith Butler</strong>, a philosopher, published <em>Gender Trouble</em> in 1990. She takes the idea of the performative utterance from the philosopher J. L. Austin (<em>How to Do Things with Words</em>, 1962): there are sentences that do not describe something, but do it, such as ‘I declare you husband and wife’ or ‘I promise to come’. Butler argues that gender works in a similar way. It does not express a prior inner essence, but is produced by repeating, from childhood, gestures, ways of speaking, dressing and moving that society assigns to each sex. Even before birth, an ultrasound and the phrase ‘it's a girl’ set in motion that chain of expectations.</p><p>There is a frequent misunderstanding that should be avoided. Performative does not mean that one chooses one's gender every morning as one chooses clothes; Butler clarified this in <em>Bodies That Matter</em> (1993). Norms precede each person and put pressure on them. But since they only exist because they are repeated, repetition can fail, deviate or be parodied. That is why, for Butler, there is a margin for change there.</p><p>Crenshaw and Butler share a suspicion: the category ‘woman’, used as if it were a homogeneous block, can leave many people out. From there, the discussions of contemporary feminism open up.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: feminism widens the question of political philosophy (who is left out of the pact?) and shows that inequality between the sexes is not natural but historical and, therefore, changeable; its currents disagree about whether the goal is equality or the recognition of difference.</strong></p>\n<p>Feminism is still alive and intersects with other problems. The <strong>ecofeminism</strong> of <strong><a href=\"#biografias/herrero\">Yayo Herrero</a></strong> (b. 1965) joins two critiques: that of <strong>patriarchy</strong> and that of an economic model that forgets two basic dependencies. We are <strong>eco-dependent</strong> (everything we consume comes from a nature with physical limits) and <strong>interdependent</strong> (no one stands alone: as babies, the sick or the elderly, someone cares for us). That work of <strong>care</strong>, done mostly by women, barely counts in the economy and yet it sustains life. That is why she proposes putting life, and not endless growth, at the centre.</p>\n<h3>Equality or difference? The reasons on each side</h3><p>You already know the distinction between the feminism of equality and that of difference. Let us now look at why each school of thought believes it is right and how this debate shows up in today’s problems.</p><p><strong>The reasons for equality.</strong> If the differences between the sexes are to a large extent constructed, insisting on them is dangerous: for centuries, ‘female nature’ served to justify women not voting, not studying or not inheriting. The safest route is universality: the same rights, the same rules and the same opportunities, because each person matters as an individual and not as a representative of a sex. A current example: in Spain, since 2021, birth leave is sixteen weeks for each parent and cannot be transferred to the other. The logic is one of equality: if only mothers enjoy it, companies will continue to see young women as risky hires.</p><p><strong>The reasons for difference.</strong> Demanding the same rights without more amounts to accepting as the measure a world designed by and for males: timetables that assume someone is caring at home and an idea of success based on competition. To become equal would be to dissolve into that mould. The psychologist Carol Gilligan (<em>In a Different Voice</em>, 1982) observed that theories of moral development, such as Kohlberg’s, scored more poorly the reasoning centred on care and relationships, which was more frequent in the women she studied, and argued that it is not an inferior morality but another voice. A current example: the demand that care be valued, counted in the economy and remunerated, rather than simply asking women to ‘leave’ it.</p><p><strong>Where they clash.</strong> Faced with quotas on electoral lists or on boards of directors, some see a temporary measure to correct an inequality and others a way of recognising that different perspectives are needed. Separate categories in competitive sport generate agreements and disagreements that do not always follow this division. The equality strand warns that celebrating ‘the feminine’ may once again confine women to the role of carers. The difference strand responds that denying all difference is accepting that the masculine is the norm.</p><p>Some authors, such as the historian Joan W. Scott in the late 1980s, argued that the dilemma is badly posed: the opposite of equality is not difference but inequality. One can be different and equal in rights.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: can an economy grow without limit on a finite planet? And who sustains, almost unseen, everyday life? It is an <strong>open debate</strong> that you will take up again in Year 2, when you reach the challenges of the 21st century; you can place Herrero in the <a href=\"#genealogias/fem\">“Women and feminism”</a> line in Genealogies.</p>\n<p>Taking the discussion even further, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/preciado\">Paul B. Preciado</a></strong> (b. 1970) continues Butler: if gender is not an essence but something that is <strong>produced</strong> — through names, clothes, expectations, images, laws, and even hormones and medicine —, then it could also be <strong>remade</strong>. He thinks of gender as a <strong>technology</strong>, not as a biological destiny. It is a debated thesis: some see in it a liberation, others fear it erases real differences. As with every political matter, we give no verdict here, but the reasons on each side.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: how much of what we call “natural” in being a man or a woman is, in fact, something learned and built by society? And where is the limit? You will debate this in Year 2; you can also place him in the <a href=\"#genealogias/fem\">“Women and feminism”</a> line in Genealogies.</p>\n<p>Feminism has not always spoken with a single voice. The German <strong><a href=\"#biografias/zetkin\">Clara Zetkin</a></strong> (1857-1933) defended a <strong>socialist feminism</strong>: for her, the oppression of women cannot be understood apart from the <strong>division into classes</strong>, and legal equality is not enough while most women depend on a wage or on unpaid work at home. Against <em>bourgeois</em> feminism, which asked above all for the vote and access to the professions, Zetkin joined the struggle for women’s rights with that of working women; she promoted, in fact, the international day that gave rise to the 8th of March. Other currents reply that gender inequality has roots of its own, not reducible to the economy.</p>\n<h3>An open debate: Nussbaum versus Butler</h3><p>In February 1999, the philosopher <strong>Martha Nussbaum</strong> published in the magazine <em>The New Republic</em> a very harsh review of Judith Butler’s work titled ‘The Professor of Parody’ (‘La profesora de la parodia’). The discussion is not only between two authors. It raises a question that applies to any movement: where is the world really changed?</p><p><strong>Nussbaum’s side.</strong> Her critique has three points. First, style: an obscure prose that, in her view, intimidates rather than argues and replaces reasons with prestige. Second, strategy: if power is everywhere and there is no way out of norms, the only thing left is to parody them, to subvert them symbolically. Nussbaum speaks of a ‘fashionable quietism’: a verbal radicalism that leaves institutions intact. Third, the lack of a normative criterion: without an idea of what is good for people, one cannot distinguish a liberating subversion from a harmful one. Against that, Nussbaum advocates a feminism of concrete reforms, such as laws against rape and harassment, access to credit or literacy, and her <strong>capabilities</strong> approach (<em>Women and Human Development</em>, 2000): asking what each woman can really do and be, for example being healthy, studying, moving about without fear or participating in decisions.</p><p><strong>Butler’s side and that of her defenders.</strong> Legal reforms are necessary, but they take place within norms that decide in advance who counts as a subject with rights. If those norms are not questioned, reform may improve the lives of some and leave out those who do not fit. Changing language is not a game: the words used to name someone influence how they are treated, whether they are believed or whether they are considered a person. Moreover, difficult thought can be valuable precisely because it forces us to doubt what seems obvious. And Butler’s later work, on precariousness and on the lives a society mourns or does not mourn, is very far from quietism.</p><p>The two positions share the goal that no one be excluded because of their sex or gender. They disagree on the path: universality and concrete measures versus suspicion of norms and cultural transformation.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: think of a recent change in your environment, such as a law, a custom or a way of speaking. Which came first, the change in words or in laws? Can one last without the other?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: is the inequality between women and men above all an economic matter, a cultural one, or both at once? Can there be real equality without also changing who has the money and the power? You will meet Clara Zetkin in Year 2; it is an <strong>open debate</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading:</strong> in <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-mill-campoamor\">Mill and Campoamor</a> you have two texts on the political equality of women: a defence of nineteenth-century liberalism and the speech with which Clara Campoamor defended women's suffrage in 1931.</p>\n<h3>The Harm Principle, in Depth</h3><p>In <em>On Liberty</em> (1859), John Stuart Mill set himself an ambitious goal: to find a single criterion that determines in which cases society is justified in intervening in what each person does, whether through laws or through the pressure of opinion. His answer is known as the <strong>Harm principle</strong>, and he formulates it in Chapter I:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>“The only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilised community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others. His own good, whether physical or moral, is not a sufficient warrant.” — J. S. Mill, <em>On Liberty</em>, ch. I</p></blockquote><p>Notice what it excludes. One may advise someone, reason with him, urge him, and even avoid his company. What one cannot do is compel him or punish him “for his own good”. In the same chapter, Mill sums it up as follows: over himself, over his own body and mind, the individual is sovereign. The principle, he clarifies, applies to adults in full possession of their faculties, not to children.</p><p>In Chapter IV, he turns this idea into a practical distinction between two kinds of conduct. One is that which <strong>concerns only yourself</strong>. The other is that which <strong>affects others</strong>, because it harms their interests. The former is outside the reach of the law; the latter can be regulated. His example is clear: no one should be punished merely for getting drunk, but a soldier or a police officer who gets drunk while on duty may be, because there other people are at stake. What is punishable is not the vice, but the neglected duty to others.</p><p>Let us test the criterion with familiar cases:</p><ul><li><strong>Motorcycle helmets.</strong> If a girl from Getxo rides without a helmet, she runs the main risk herself. With Mill in hand, it would be difficult to justify a legal obligation, unless harm to third parties can be demonstrated.</li><li><strong>Alcohol.</strong> Drinking at home on a Saturday falls within what concerns only yourself; driving afterwards does not. The principle does not prohibit beer: it prohibits endangering pedestrians.</li><li><strong>Online gambling.</strong> An adult who gambles his own money decides over what is his. But if what he loses is his family's rent money, his conduct begins to affect others, and the question changes.</li></ul><p>The appeal of the principle lies in its simplicity: it does not require the State to know what life is good for each person, only that it prevents some from harming others. Its difficulty, as we shall see, lies in the word that underpins it all: what counts as harm?</p>\n<h3>Objections: What Is Harm and Who Decides?</h3><p>The first criticism is that Mill never precisely defines what a <strong>harm</strong> is. Only physical harm? Also economic, psychological, or harm to one's reputation? Depending on where we draw the line, the principle protects a great deal or almost nothing.</p><p>The second criticism goes further: if we count <strong>indirect harms</strong>, almost no conduct is purely private. The motorcyclist without a helmet who has an accident occupies a hospital bed that we all pay for and may leave his family without support. Whoever is ruined by gambling drags down their own. If everything affects someone, the boundary of Chapter IV dissolves.</p><p>And there remains a third: who decides what harm is? If the majority decides it, the principle that was meant to protect the individual from it ends up in its hands.</p><p>From those cracks emerged two corrections. The first is the <strong>offence principle</strong>, which the American philosopher Joel Feinberg defended in <em>Offense to Others</em> (1985). According to him, it is sometimes justified to prohibit conduct that does not harm, but that seriously offends others. To decide this, one must weigh several things: how intense and lasting the nuisance is, whether it can be easily avoided, and what value the conduct has for the person who engages in it.</p><p>The second is the analysis of <strong>paternalism</strong> that Gerald Dworkin made in the article “Paternalism” (1972). Paternalism consists in limiting someone's freedom for reasons that look only to their own good. Today two types are usually distinguished:</p><ul><li><strong>soft paternalism</strong> intervenes when we doubt that the person is truly deciding freely, because they are uninformed, a minor, or under pressure;</li><li><strong>hard paternalism</strong> intervenes even if the decision is free and informed.</li></ul><p>Mill himself admitted something similar to the first: if someone is about to cross a bridge that is collapsing and there is no time to warn them, they may be stopped, because no one wants to fall into the river (ch. V).</p><p>There are three cases in which the disagreement is real:</p><ul><li><strong>Hate speech on social media.</strong> Some argue that it is not merely an offence: it targets groups, prepares the ground for discrimination and aggression, and drives out of the debate those it attacks. Others respond that punishment should be reserved for direct threats or incitement, and that giving the State or the platforms the power to decide what is hate opens the door to silencing uncomfortable opinions.</li><li><strong>Drug use.</strong> In favour of prohibiting it, it is said that addiction undermines one's own freedom and that its effects reach families and neighbourhoods. Against it, it is said that the adult decides over their body, and that prohibition generates illegal markets and punishes above all the most vulnerable.</li><li><strong>Mandatory vaccination.</strong> Here the harm to third parties is clearer, because someone who is not vaccinated can infect someone who cannot protect themselves. But others recall that forcing intervention in one's own body is a very strong measure, and prefer to inform, recommend, and incentivise.</li></ul>\n<h3>Mill Responds, and the Debate Continues</h3><p>A defender of Mill could answer these objections with Chapter II, the most famous of the book, devoted to <strong>freedom of thought and discussion</strong>. There one sees that his principle does not stem from indifference, but from a thesis about how knowledge advances. The starting point is forceful:</p><blockquote class=\"src\"><p>“If all mankind minus one were of one opinion, and only one person were of the contrary opinion, mankind would be no more justified in silencing that one person than he, if he had the power, would be justified in silencing mankind.” — J. S. Mill, <em>On Liberty</em>, ch. II</p></blockquote><p>Mill offers several interlocking reasons:</p><ul><li>If the silenced opinion is true, we lose the opportunity to correct an error.</li><li>If it is false, it often contains a part of truth that the dominant opinion had forgotten.</li><li>And even if it were entirely false, discussing it forces those who are right to know <em>why</em> they are right.</li></ul><p>A truth that no one tests is repeated like a prejudice, without being understood, and ends up turned into a dead dogma instead of a living truth. Think of someone who believes that the Earth is round only because “everyone says so”: faced with the first well-made flat-earth video, they would not know what to answer. Someone who has had to refute it would.</p><p>Hence the political conclusion that Mill already anticipated in Chapter I: no society that does not respect these liberties as a whole is free, whatever its form of government. A voting majority can be as oppressive as a tyrant if it punishes those who think differently.</p><p>But the debate does not close:</p><ul><li>Those who defend Mill maintain that the answer to a bad idea is a better idea, not censorship, and that whoever can prohibit error can also prohibit the truth that bothers them.</li><li>His critics reply that Mill was thinking of a debate among reasonable people, in books and newspapers. He was not thinking of social networks where lies spread faster than their rebuttal, algorithms reward the extreme, and mass harassment can silence someone without any law intervening.</li><li>Others add that not everyone arrives at public discussion with the same voice or the same loudspeaker.</li></ul><p>To that it can be replied, in turn, that the remedy is not less freedom, but more education and more critical thinking. What is at stake is where one person's freedom ends and where harm to another begins.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can you think of any conduct that affects only you, with no effect on anyone else? If you find one, should the State be able to prohibit it for your own good? And if you do not find one, does that mean that everything can be regulated, or that a threshold of harm must be set from which to intervene? In that case, who should set it?</p>\n<p>A quiet thread links these authors: <strong>Wilhelm von Humboldt</strong> (1767-1835). In <em>The Limits of State Action</em> he argued that the end of the person is to develop their faculties to the full and in a harmonious way, their <strong>individuality</strong>, and that the state should intervene only as much as is needed so as not to stifle it. Mill admired that idea so much that he placed it as the epigraph to <em>On Liberty</em>. And from there springs an argument that Campoamor would carry onto political ground: if the supreme value is the free development of each individual, there is no reason to deny it to half the population for being women. The defence of individual liberty thus leads to the defence of political equality.</p>\n<h2>13. Marx: the critique of capitalist society</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-93\">The contractualists explained the State as a pact between free and equal individuals. <strong>Karl Marx</strong> (1818-1883) is suspicious of that picture: in a society divided between those who own the factories and the land and those who have only their labour to live on, nobody makes a pact as an equal. So he asks not so much which State would be legitimate as <strong>whom the State that exists serves</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Food for thought: if two people sign a work contract and one of them has no other way of eating, did both sign freely?</p>\n<p>His central ideas, which you will study in more detail in History of Philosophy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Historical materialism.</strong> The base of a society is its way of producing: who works, who owns the means of production and how what is produced is shared out. On that economic <strong>structure</strong> rises a <strong>superstructure</strong>: laws, politics, religion, ideas. ‘It is not the consciousness of men that determines their being, but, on the contrary, their social being that determines their consciousness’ (<em>A Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy</em>, Preface, 1859).</li>\n<li><strong>Class struggle.</strong> ‘The history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles’ (Marx and Engels, <em>The Communist Manifesto</em>, ch. I, 1848): masters and slaves, lords and serfs, and, under capitalism, the <strong>bourgeoisie</strong> and the <strong>proletariat</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exploitation and surplus value.</strong> In a working day the worker produces more value than he receives as a wage; that difference, surplus value, is kept by the capitalist (<em>Capital</em>, 1867).</li>\n<li><strong>Alienation.</strong> In wage labour, the worker becomes a stranger to what he produces, to his own activity and to other people: he works to survive, not to fulfil himself (<em>Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts of 1844</em>, 1844).</li>\n<li><strong>The state is not neutral.</strong> ‘The executive of the modern state is but a committee for managing the common affairs of the whole bourgeoisie’ (<em>The Communist Manifesto</em>, ch. I). Laws and the police protect, above all, the property of those who already own.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Liberalism (Locke)</th><th>Marx</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>What the state is</strong></td><td>A referee that protects everyone’s rights.</td><td>An instrument of the ruling class.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Property</strong></td><td>A natural right that the State must guarantee.</td><td>Private ownership of the means of production is the origin of exploitation.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Freedom</strong></td><td>That nobody interferes in my life.</td><td>Merely formal if there are no material conditions for exercising it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>The goal</strong></td><td>To limit the power of the State.</td><td>To overcome classes; the State would end up ‘withering away’ (Engels, <em>Anti-Dühring</em>, 1878).</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Marx wanted something more than to explain: ‘The philosophers have only interpreted the world in various ways; the point is to change it’ (<em>Theses on Feuerbach</em>, XI, 1845). He proposed a revolution of the proletariat that would collectivise the means of production and open the way to a classless society.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘religion is the opium of the people’ is quoted as an insult, but the full text says more: religion is ‘the sigh of the oppressed creature, the heart of a heartless world’ and, for that reason, ‘the opium of the people’ (<em>Contribution to the Critique of Hegel’s Philosophy of Right: Introduction</em>, 1844). Marx is not mocking the believer: he is saying that religion consoles for real suffering and that this suffering must be brought to an end.</p>\n<h3>Does Marx lead to the Gulag? An open debate</h3>\n<p><strong>Two faces of civil society.</strong> Between the family and the State there is an enormous space of relations: buying, working, associating, helping. It is called civil society, and it can be understood in two ways.</p><ul><li><strong>As market.</strong> Hegel, in his <em>Elements of the Philosophy of Right</em> (§ 182 ff.), describes civil society as the place where each individual takes himself as an end and treats others as means to achieve it. The curious thing is that an order arises from this, the “system of needs”: in order to satisfy my needs I have to work to satisfy those of others, and thus everyone becomes intertwined. It is an idea close to that of Adam Smith, which you have already seen.</li><li><strong>As third sector.</strong> Associations, NGOs, volunteering or cooperatives do not seek individual benefit, but shared ends: caring, defending a cause, sustaining a common project. Here the other is not a means, but a companion.</li></ul><p>Which is better? The decisive criterion lies not in the form, but in <strong>consent</strong>. A market exchange is legitimate if both parties accept it freely; a solidarity association also ceases to be so if it forces people to participate against their will. Remember the question from the section on Marx: someone who signs a contract because they have no other way to eat, do they truly consent?</p><p>Compare the corner shop, where the owner sells fruit to make a living and you buy it because it suits you, with the food bank organised by school volunteers for families who need it. In the first, each seeks their own and both come out winning; in the second, no one seeks anything for themselves. Both relationships are valuable if no one is obliged.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can the market work well without a prior basis of trust and solidarity? Would you buy something from someone you do not trust at all?</p>\n<p>Marx died in 1883, thirty-four years before the Russian Revolution (1917), and he barely described what society after capitalism would be like. Whether or not his ideas lead to regimes such as Stalin’s is a question that divides scholars:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>See a connection</th><th>See a distortion or a betrayal</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Karl Popper</strong> (<em>The Open Society and Its Enemies</em>, 1945): Marx believed in laws of history that make it possible to predict the future, and that belief can justify sacrificing the people of today in the name of a future society.</td><td><strong>Rosa Luxemburg</strong> (<em>The Russian Revolution</em>, written in 1918 and published in 1922, after her death), a Marxist, criticised from the outset the fact that Lenin and Trotsky suppressed the elected assembly and rival parties: ‘Freedom is always the freedom of the one who thinks differently’.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Leszek Kołakowski</strong> (<em>Main Currents of Marxism</em>, 1976-1978), a former Polish Marxist: Stalinism was one of the possible interpretations of Marxism, though neither the only one nor the inevitable one.</td><td><strong>Leon Trotsky</strong> (<em>The Revolution Betrayed</em>, 1936): a bureaucracy seized the revolution. The <strong>Frankfurt School</strong> (Herbert Marcuse, <em>Soviet Marxism</em>, 1958) and <strong>humanist Marxism</strong> (Erich Fromm, <em>Marx’s Concept of Man</em>, 1961) set against official Soviet Marxism a Marx of freedom and emancipation.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Hannah Arendt</strong> holds a place of her own. In some unfinished notes on Marx (around 1953) she distinguishes him from Stalin and reads the problem as something broader: in her view, what is disturbing in Marx comes from the whole tradition of Western political thought that he inherited and carried to the extreme. She was concerned, for example, by the idea that violence is ‘the midwife of every old society pregnant with a new one’ (<em>Capital</em>, Book I, ch. 24) or that history is ‘made’ in the way an object is manufactured. On the concept of ‘totalitarianism’ itself and its use in the Cold War, see the next section.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Food for thought: can a theory be judged by what was done by those who claimed to follow it? Think of other cases: is Christianity responsible for the Inquisition, or liberalism for colonialism? What would have to happen for the answer to be ‘yes’?</p>\n<p>The different Marxisms (Engels, Lenin, Luxemburg, Gramsci, the Frankfurt School and others) and Marx’s legacy in the sciences are surveyed in the exploration ‘Marxisms and Marx’s Legacy in the Sciences’.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: for Marx, politics cannot be understood without economics. As long as society is divided into classes, the state defends the interests of the class that owns the means of production, and other people’s freedom is largely formal.</strong></p>\n<p>The critique of the economy leads to a question that seems simple: what is <strong>money</strong>? The British sociologist <strong><a href=\"#biografias/ingham\">Geoffrey Ingham</a></strong> (b. 1942) argues that money is not just a commodity useful for not having to swap wheat for shoes, as is usually told. For him, money is above all a <strong>social relation</strong>: a promise of payment backed by trust and by power (the State, the banks), a form of <strong>credit and debt</strong> that organises who owes whom. That is why whoever controls the creation of money holds enormous power. Other economists reply that money arose above all to make exchange easier, and that its practical function is what matters.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: is money just a neutral tool for buying and selling, or a form of power that decides who rules over whom? Who should be able to «create» money? You will meet Geoffrey Ingham in Year 2; it is an <strong>open debate</strong>.</p>\n<h2>14. The Totalitarianisms of the 20th Century: A Contested Concept</h2>\n<p>In the first half of the 20th century, regimes appeared that were not content merely to rule: they also wanted to control thought and private life. The term was born in Mussolini’s Italy, which adopted it with pride: ‘Everything within the State, nothing outside the State, nothing against the State’ (speeches of 1925). Italian <strong>fascism</strong> (from 1922) and German <strong>Nazism</strong> (1933–1945) are the clearest cases. Whether Soviet <strong>Stalinism</strong> belongs to the same category, on the other hand, is one of the great debates in historiography.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Food for thought: what is the difference between a government that forbids you to criticise it and one that also wants to decide what you think, what you read and whom you love?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-112\"><strong>Hannah Arendt</strong>, a German Jew who had to flee Nazism, proposed in <em>The Origins of Totalitarianism</em> (1951) that Nazism and Stalinism were two versions of a new form of power. Carl Friedrich and Zbigniew Brzezinski summed it up in common features (1956):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A single party and a leader</strong> to whom unlimited authority is attributed.</li>\n<li><strong>An ideology that claims to explain everything</strong> and does not admit what does not fit into it.</li>\n<li><strong>Terror</strong>: secret police, denunciation and camps.</li>\n<li><strong>Propaganda and control of information</strong>: press, radio, school and cinema at the service of the regime.</li>\n<li><strong>The destruction of private life and of plurality</strong>: isolated individuals, with no free associations or public space.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The facts are not in dispute: the Nazi Holocaust murdered some six million Jews, as well as Roma, disabled people, homosexuals and opponents; in the Soviet Gulag, according to the Soviet archives themselves, around one and a half million prisoners died, to whom are added the victims of the Great Terror (1937-1938) and of the famines. What is disputed is whether a single category explains such different regimes well.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Those who compare the two regimes</th><th>Those who criticise the comparison</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Arendt</strong> (1951) and <strong>Friedrich and Brzezinski</strong> (1956): a single form of total domination.</td><td><strong>Ian Kershaw and Moshe Lewin</strong> (<em>Stalinism and Nazism</em>, 1997): when compared in detail, differences above all appear; for example, Stalin intervened ceaselessly in the administration, whereas Hitler kept away from it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>François Furet</strong> (<em>The Passing of an Illusion</em>, 1995): fascism and communism, two sister utopias against liberal democracy.</td><td>The ‘social history’ of the USSR (<strong>Sheila Fitzpatrick</strong>; <strong>J. Arch Getty</strong>, 1985): the Soviet state was less monolithic than the model supposed; there was disorder, local initiative and social support. Fitzpatrick and Geyer (<em>Beyond Totalitarianism</em>, 2009) conclude that the concept is of little use for analysing either regime.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Timothy Snyder</strong> (<em>Bloodlands</em>, 2010): studies the massacres of both together in Eastern Europe; to forbid the comparison would be to falsify the lives of the victims.</td><td><strong>Enzo Traverso</strong> (<em>Totalitarianism: History of a Debate</em>, 2001): the concept was used in the Cold War as a political weapon to equate communism and Nazism. <strong>Domenico Losurdo</strong> (2008) criticises Arendt’s equation, although his own work is widely disputed for playing down Stalin’s crimes.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two differences are most often pointed out. The first is the <strong>goal</strong>: Nazism was based on an ideology of racial hierarchy that made extermination an end in itself, whereas Soviet communism invoked a universalist ideal of emancipation, which its crimes contradicted. The second is the <strong>uniqueness of the Holocaust</strong>: in the German ‘historians’ dispute’ (1986), Ernst Nolte presented Nazi crimes as a reaction to Bolshevik terror, and Jürgen Habermas accused him of using the comparison to relativise the extermination of the Jews.</p>\n<p>Arendt added, moreover, an idea that does not depend on this debate: after attending the trial of Adolf Eichmann, one of the organisers of the deportations, she spoke of the <strong>banality of evil</strong> (<em>Eichmann in Jerusalem</em>, 1963). Eichmann did not strike her as a monster but as an obedient official who did not think about what he was doing. Great crimes need many ordinary people who stop judging for themselves; that is why thinking critically is also a form of resistance.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Authoritarian regime</th><th>Totalitarian regime</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>What it controls</strong></td><td>Politics: it bans parties and punishes opposition.</td><td>Everything: politics, the economy, culture and private life.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>What it asks of the population</strong></td><td>Obedience and passivity.</td><td>Enthusiastic support and permanent mobilisation.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Ideology</strong></td><td>It can be vague or traditional.</td><td>Total: an explanation of the whole of reality.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> comparing is not equating. Comparing two regimes serves to see both what they share and what separates them; whoever compares Nazism and Stalinism is not thereby saying that they were the same, and whoever criticises the comparison is not denying Stalin’s crimes. And two further traps: ‘totalitarian’ is not a synonym for ‘dictatorship’ (the case of Francoism, in the next section), and totalitarianisms did not always come to power by force: Hitler gained power in 1933 by legal means and then turned it into a dictatorship.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: ‘totalitarianism’ names the form of power that leaves nothing outside its control, not even thought; fascism and Nazism are its clearest cases. Whether Stalinism fits into the same category is an open debate among historians, and it is worth knowing each side’s reasons.</strong></p>\n<h2>15. Was Francoism totalitarian?</h2>\n<p>The dictatorship of Francisco Franco was born of a civil war (1936-1939) and lasted until the dictator’s death in 1975. The facts of the repression are documented: during the war some 200,000 people were murdered far from the front, the great majority of them in the rebel zone (Paul Preston, <em>The Spanish Holocaust</em>, 2011), and thousands more were executed in the post-war period; Javier Rodrigo catalogued in 2005 almost two hundred Francoist concentration camps, and later studies raise the figure to nearly three hundred. What is disputed is <strong>what kind of regime</strong> it was.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Food for thought: does it matter what we call a regime if we already know what it did? What changes when we say ‘authoritarian’, ‘fascist’ or ‘totalitarian’?</p>\n<p>The regime itself defined itself in this way at the outset. The Labour Charter (Fuero del Trabajo, 9 March 1938) declared in its preamble a State that was ‘National in that it is a totalitarian instrument at the service of the integrity of the fatherland’. In 1967, the Organic Law of the State rewrote that preamble and the word disappeared: after the defeat of fascism in 1945, the dictatorship had been erasing its fascist features in order to present itself as Catholic, traditional and, later, technocratic.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Authoritarian regime</th><th>Fascist or totalitarian regime, at least at the beginning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Juan J. Linz</strong> (1964): an authoritarian regime, with limited pluralism (the Falange, the Church, the Army, the monarchists), ‘mentalities’ rather than a closed ideology, and little mobilisation of the population.</td><td><strong>Manuel Tuñón de Lara</strong> and <strong>Raúl Morodo</strong>: Linz’s thesis ‘beautifies’ Francoism. <strong>Joan Martínez Alier</strong>: the lack of mobilisation was not a feature of the regime, but the result of repression.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Stanley G. Payne</strong>: an authoritarian dictatorship with a first ‘semi-fascist’ phase (1936–1945) that later changed.</td><td><strong>Ismael Saz</strong> (1993): a ‘fascistised’ regime. <strong>Josep Fontana</strong> places its fascist essence in the first stage; <strong>Ignacio Sotelo</strong> speaks of a ‘fascism of underdevelopment’.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Javier Tusell</strong> (<em>La dictadura de Franco</em>, ‘Franco’s Dictatorship’, 1988), whose book is considered to have all but closed the debate in favour of the authoritarian thesis.</td><td><strong>Manuel Ramírez</strong> distinguishes three stages: a first ‘totalitarian regime’, a conservative dictatorship and a technocratic Francoism.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>An unfinished task.</strong> Democracy is not a trophy that is won once and then kept in a display cabinet. It is never complete or guaranteed: its institutions can age, its inequalities can exclude part of the citizenry, and the trust that sustains it can wear thin. For this reason, it demands constant self-criticism. Karl Popper, in <em>The Open Society and Its Enemies</em> (1945), argued that the important question is not who should govern, but how to organise institutions so that bad rulers can do the least possible harm. For him, what is valuable about democracy is that it allows one to get rid of a government without shedding blood and to correct mistakes as one goes along.</p><p>Among the evils that harm it most is <strong>corruption</strong>, which acts like gangrene in the rule of law: it starts at one point and, if it is not cut out, it spreads. It breaks equality before the law, because some obtain what others are denied; it diverts public resources, which stop reaching schools or health centres; and it erodes trust, because those who discover it begin to think that “they are all the same” and lose interest in the common good.</p><p>How is it fought? With <strong>transparency</strong> (access to information and accountability), with <strong>independent oversight</strong> (courts, audits, a free press) and with <strong>efficient administrations</strong>, with clear procedures, in which no one needs a “contact” in order to be attended to.</p><p>A town council has to renovate a sports centre. In one case, it calls for public tenders, sets the criteria in advance and publishes all the offers received. In another, the councillor commissions the work from a friend’s company without asking anyone else. The material result may be similar, but only in the first case can the citizenry verify that it has been done properly.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: jumping a queue, cheating in an exam, asking an acquaintance to “fast-track” a procedure for you… Is the small everyday trick a form of corruption, or is there a difference in kind between it and grand corruption?</p>\n<p>There were also attempts to get beyond that alternative: Salvador Giner, Eduardo Sevilla Guzmán and Manuel Pérez Yruela (1978) proposed considering Francoism a ‘<strong>modern despotism</strong>’, a type of dictatorship of its own that does not fit entirely into either fascism or simple authoritarianism.</p>\n<p>Many historians agree on <strong>distinguishing stages</strong>: an early Francoism (1939-1945) with a single party, a cult of the leader, trade-union regimentation and clearly fascist or totalitarian features, and a later evolution towards a conservative dictatorship that sought passive obedience rather than enthusiastic support. The disagreement is over which stage defines the regime best and over whether the word ‘authoritarian’ describes or softens what it was.</p>\n<h3>Intellectuals before the war</h3>\n<p>The question of Spain and its backwardness occupied several generations of intellectuals. After the defeat of 1898, the <strong>regenerationism</strong> of Joaquín Costa summed up its programme in ‘school and larder’, and the <strong>Generation of ’98</strong> (Unamuno, Baroja, Antonio Machado and, in his youth, Maeztu) asked about the being of Spain. The <strong>Institución Libre de Enseñanza</strong> (Free Institution of Education; Francisco Giner de los Ríos, 1876), the <strong>Junta para Ampliación de Estudios</strong> (Board for the Advancement of Studies; 1907) and the <strong>Residencia de Estudiantes</strong> (Students’ Residence; 1910) sought to modernise the country through education and science.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t6-130\"><strong>José Ortega y Gasset</strong> took that ambition into politics: he founded the League for Political Education (1913); in ‘The Berenguer Error’ (<em>El Sol</em>, 1930) he ended with a ‘<em>Delenda est Monarchia</em>’; and in 1931 he created, with Gregorio Marañón and Ramón Pérez de Ayala, the Group at the Service of the Republic. The Second Republic took books, theatre and cinema to the villages with the <strong>Pedagogical Missions</strong>, in which María Zambrano took part, and in the Cortes of 1931 Clara Campoamor defended votes for women against Victoria Kent, who asked for it to be postponed.</p>\n<p>Not all intellectuals were liberals or left-wing. Ramiro de Maeztu promoted <em>Acción Española</em> (1931), the core of Catholic counter-revolutionary thought, and Ernesto Giménez Caballero and Ramiro Ledesma Ramos spread fascist ideas. Once the war had begun, Federico García Lorca was murdered in August 1936, and on 12 October of that year Unamuno confronted General Millán-Astray at the University of Salamanca. The phrase ‘you will win, but you will not convince’ comes from a later literary re-creation (Luis Portillo, 1941, who was not present): it is not known exactly what Unamuno said.</p>\n<h3>Intellectuals under the dictatorship</h3>\n<p>After the war, thousands of teachers were <strong>purged</strong> (in some provinces, more than a third) and many intellectuals went into <strong>exile</strong>: Antonio Machado died in Collioure in 1939; María Zambrano lived in the Americas and in Europe; José Gaos, who reached Mexico in 1938, called himself ‘transterrado’ (‘transplanted’); Francisco Ayala taught in Argentina and Puerto Rico. Inside Spain, the regime relied on <strong>National Catholicism</strong>, and a group of Falangist intellectuals (Pedro Laín Entralgo, Antonio Tovar, Dionisio Ridruejo and the journal <em>Escorial</em>, 1940) sought to give the new State a culture of its own; over the years, several of them distanced themselves from the regime.</p>\n<p>The polemic of 1949 pitted two attitudes against each other: Laín, in <em>España como problema</em> (Spain as a Problem), wanted to integrate the liberal tradition from before the war; Rafael Calvo Serer answered him with <em>España sin problema</em> (Spain without a Problem), for whom the Catholic tradition was already the solution. Ortega, back from exile but kept apart from official Spain, founded with Julián Marías the <strong>Institute of Humanities</strong> (1948), a private institution outside the university.</p>\n<p>From the 1950s critical voices grew: the <strong>university incidents of 1956</strong> in Madrid, with students and young intellectuals arrested (among them Ridruejo, by then distanced from the regime); the Munich meeting of 1962, at which opponents from inside Spain and from exile called for democracy and which the regime labelled a ‘contubernio’ (‘conspiracy’); the journal <em>Cuadernos para el Diálogo</em> (1963), founded by Joaquín Ruiz-Giménez; and, in 1965, the removal from their chairs of José Luis López Aranguren, Enrique Tierno Galván, Agustín García Calvo and Santiago Montero Díaz for supporting the student protests, while José María Valverde and Antonio Tovar resigned theirs in solidarity.</p>\n<p>Intellectuals served, then, to <strong>legitimise</strong> the regime, above all in its early years, when it wished to mobilise an ideology of its own, and to <strong>erode</strong> it, from exile and, increasingly, from within.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Food for thought: what does it say about a regime that many of those who built its ideology ended up criticising it? Does it bring it closer to the totalitarian model, which needed intellectuals to justify it, or does it move it further away, because such criticism came to exist inside the country, even if it was punished?</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘authoritarian’ does not mean ‘soft’. Linz’s distinction concerns how power was organised, not how much harm it did: an authoritarian regime can be as repressive as a totalitarian one. And the other way round: whoever calls early Francoism totalitarian is not thereby claiming that it was the same as Nazism.</p>\n<p><strong>Key idea: Francoism called itself ‘totalitarian’ in 1938 and erased the word in 1967. For some historians it was an authoritarian regime with a fascist phase; for others, a fascist or totalitarian dictatorship, at least in its early years. The repression is not in dispute: what is disputed is the category that best explains it.</strong></p>"}});
